{"type": "tool_call", "id": "tool-receipt:2bb314113beae3a139b56120", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108"}, "result": "## Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\n**Source**: https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108\n\n---\n\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit![Learn more](https://info.arxiv.org/about)\n\n[License: CC Zero](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2408.16108v1 [cs.DS] 28 Aug 2024\n\n# Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\nAntoine JouxNote:CISPA Helmholtz Center for Information Security, Germany,joux@cipa.de. This work has been supported by the European Union’s H2020 Programme under grant agreement number ERC-669891.Karol WęgrzyckiNote:Saarland University and Max Planck Institute for Informatics, Saarbrücken, Germany,wegrzycki@cs.uni-saarland.de. This work is part of the project TIPEA that has received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement No 850979).\n\n###### Abstract\n\nLagarias and Odlyzko (J. ACM 1985) proposed a polynomial time algorithm for solving “*almost all*” instances of the Subset Sum problem withnnintegers of sizeΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}), wherelog2⁡(ΓLO)>n2​log2⁡(γ)\\log_{2}(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})>n^{2}\\log_{2}(\\gamma)andγ\\gammais a parameter of the lattice basis reduction (γ>4/3\\gamma>\\sqrt{4/3}for LLL). The algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is a cornerstone result in cryptography. However, the theoretical guarantee on the density of feasible instances has remained unimproved for almost 40 years.\n\nIn this paper, we propose an algorithm to solve “*almost all*” instances of Subset Sum with integers of sizeΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}})after a single call to the lattice reduction. Additionally, our argument allows us to solve the Subset Sum problem for multiple targets while the previous approach could only answer one target per call to lattice basis reduction. We introduce a modular arithmetic approach to the Subset Sum problem. The idea is to use the lattice reduction to solve a linear system modulo a suitably large prime. We show that density guarantees can be improved, by analysing the lengths of the LLL reduced basis vectors, of both the primal and the dual lattices simultaneously.\n\n## 1Introduction\n\nThe Subset Sum problem, a cornerstone of computational complexity and cryptography, has been the subject of extensive research due to numerous practical implications. In the Subset Sum problem, we are givennnpositive integersa1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}and a targetT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}. The task is to decide if there exists a subset of the given numbers that sums toTT.\n\nIn particular, the study of Subset Sum has seen numerous applications in cryptography[[36](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib36),[18](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib18)], balancing[[15](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib15)], and combinatorial optimization[[29](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib29)]. With these applications in mind, our goal is to propose an efficient algorithm to solve the Subset Sum for a large range of instances. Indeed, since the problem is NP-hard, it is very unlikely that we could propose an efficient algorithm to solve all instances of the problem. The natural next step is to characterize which instances of the problem can be solved efficiently. On that front, there is a classical result of Bellman[[9](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib9)]that proposes a polynomial time algorithm for Subset Sum when the numbers are polynomially bounded, i.e., the instance is*dense*. This inspired a long line of pseudopolynomial-time algorithms (see e.g.,[[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib12),[30](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib30),[1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib1),[40](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib40),[34](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib34)]).\n\nOn the other end of the spectrum, there is a result by Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]for the*sparse*instances. This work marked a significant milestone by proposing a polynomial-time algorithm capable of solving “*almost all*” instances of Subset Sum when the size of the numbers isΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}), wherelog2⁡(ΓLO)>n2​log2​γ\\log_{2}(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})>n^{2}\\log_{2}{\\gamma}andγ\\gammais a parameter associated with the lattice reduction. Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]used the LLL (Lenstra-Lenstra-Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]) algorithm as a lattice basis reduction, for whichγ=γLLL>4/3\\gamma=\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}.\n\nTo this day, the algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is a cornerstone of modern cryptography and is featured in numerous text-books on the field[[21](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib21),[10](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib10),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)]. This algorithm was subsequently implemented[[41](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib41),[44](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib44),[11](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib11)], simplified[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]and even improved assuming access to an exact CVP[[17](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib17),[28](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib28)]. Nevertheless, the algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is currently the only known method111There are several extensions of the LLL algorithm tailored for solving Subset Sum in practice, see[[44](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib44),[32](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib32)].to solve low-density instances of Subset Sum, both in theory and practice (see[[39](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib39)]for survey).\n\nIn this paper, we revisit and improve the result of Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]. We show an algorithm solving almost all instances of Subset Sum when the size of the numbers isΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}). More formally, we prove the following theorem.\n\n###### Theorem 1.1.\n\nLeta1,…,an∈ℤ⩾0a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{\\geqslant 0}be integers, selected uniformly at random from{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}, whereΓLO=(γLLL)n2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}=(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}. With overwhelming probability, after a single call to a basis reduction algorithm with parameterγLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}, we obtain a polynomial time tester, that for every givenT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}decides whetherthere exist​e1,…,en∈{0,1}​such that​∑i=1nei⋅ai=T.\\text{there exist }e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ such that }\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}\\cdot a_{i}=T.\n\nOur tester and its creation method are both deterministic. Their success probability only depends on the random choice of the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}and is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nThe improvement offered by[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)is two-fold. First, in terms of admissible range, our algorithm provides improvement fromΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})down toΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}})on numbers in the instance. Second, our algorithm is*oblivious*to the target. Namely, after a single call to the lattice basis reduction, with input numbersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}we can deterministically decide on an answer in polynomial time. In that sense, our argument provides a succinct certificate for “almost all” instances of sparse Subset Sum. Precisely this question was already considered by Furst and Kannan[[20](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib20)]who used lattice basis reduction to bound the proof complexity of Subset Sum. They strengthened the Lagarias and Odlyzko algorithm and showed that “almost all” instances with numbers in{1,…,ΓLO}\\{1,\\ldots,\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}\\}have a polynomial size proof. Our argument again reduces the range down to{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}, and our certificates are simpler. In fact, the data-structure behind[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)invokes a single matrix-vector multiplication.\n\n### 1.1Our Technique: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\nNow, we elaborate on the techniques that we introduce. In this note, we let⟦a⟧p\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{p}be a unique integer in(−p/2,p/2)(-p/2,p/2)such thata≡⟦a⟧p​(mod​p)a\\equiv\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{p}\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ p)(see[Section2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2)for notation). Consider vectorsa=(a1,…,an)∈ℤn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}=(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n},e=(e1,…,en)∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}and an integerTTsuch thate1⋅a1+e2⋅a2+…+en⋅an\\displaystyle e_{1}\\cdot a_{1}+e_{2}\\cdot a_{2}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot a_{n}=T.\\displaystyle=T.Obviously, for any integersμ,p∈ℤ\\mu,p\\in\\mathbb{Z}the following also holdse1⋅⟦μ​a1⟧p+e2⋅⟦μ​a2⟧p+…+en⋅⟦μ​an⟧p\\displaystyle e_{1}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}+e_{2}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}≡⟦μ​T⟧p​(mod​p).\\displaystyle\\equiv\\llbracket\\mu T\\rrbracket_{p}\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ p).\n\nNext, assume, that we have managed to findμ\\muandpp, such that:∑i=1n|⟦μ​ai⟧p|<p/2.\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\\llbracket\\mu a_{i}\\rrbracket_{p}|<p/2.\n\nThen there are no overflows moduloppand actually, we can writee1⋅⟦μ​a1⟧p+e2⋅⟦μ​a2⟧p+…+en⋅⟦μ​an⟧p=⟦μ​T⟧p.e_{1}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}+e_{2}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}=\\llbracket\\mu T\\rrbracket_{p}.\n\nWe will show that if the density is small, then there are integersμ1,…,μn∈ℤ\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that for each of them*(a)*theℓ1\\ell_{1}norm is bounded, i.e.,‖⟦μi​a⟧p‖1<p/2\\left\\lVert\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}\\right\\rVert_{1}<p/2, and*(b)*the vectors⟦μi​a⟧p\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}are linearly independent. More formally we will prove the following theorem.\n\n###### Theorem 1.2(Modular Arithmetic Approach).\n\nLetΓLO≔Θ⁡((γLLL)n2⋅24​n​log2⁡n)\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}\\coloneqq\\Theta((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{4n\\log_{2}{n}})and letp=Θ⁡((γLLL)0.5​n2)p=\\Theta((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{0.5n^{2}})be a prime. There exists a polynomial time algorithm, that given integersa1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}selected uniformly at random from{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}outputs, with overwhelming probability, integersμ1,…,μn∈ℤ\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that the followingn×nn\\times nmatrixℳp≔(⟦μ1​a1⟧p⟦μ1​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ1​an⟧p⟦μ2​a1⟧p⟦μ2​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ2​an⟧p⟦μn​a1⟧p⟦μn​a2⟧p⋯⟦μn​an⟧p)\\mathcal{M}_{p}\\coloneqq\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots&&&\\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\n\nhas full rank. Moreover, for every1⩽i⩽n1\\leqslant i\\leqslant nit holds that:‖(⟦μi​a1⟧p,…,⟦μi​an⟧p)‖1<p2.\\left\\lVert(\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})\\right\\rVert_{1}<\\frac{p}{2}.\n\nMore precisely, the procedure succeeds with probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}, which only depends on the random choice of integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}.\n\nObserve, that the algorithm for[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)follows easily with the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}in hand. Namely, considerℳp⋅e⊺=t\\mathcal{M}_{p}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}^{\\intercal}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}, wheret≔(⟦μ1​T⟧p,…,⟦μn​T⟧p)⊺{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}\\coloneqq(\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p})^{\\intercal}. First, because theℓ1\\ell_{1}norm of each row ofℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}is bounded byp/2p/2, vectoreis also a solution to⟨a,e⟩=T\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\rangle=T. Second, by the fact thatℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full-rank, such a vectoreis*unique*. Hence, it is enough to compute the inverse ofℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}(which is possible becauseℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full-rank) and verify that the vector(ℳp)−1⋅t(\\mathcal{M}_{p})^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}is in{0,1}n\\{0,1\\}^{n}(see[Section4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4)for the full proof).\n\nNow, we briefly sketch the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). Our algorithm is inspired by Howgrave-Graham’s revisit[[25](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib25)]of Coppersmith’s method for finding short roots of univariate modular equations[[16](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib16)]. In a nutshell, we construct a lattice (very similar to the original lattice of Lagarias and Odlyzko) for Subset Sum modulo a prime numberpp. Analogously to the original approach of Lagarias and Odlyzko we bound the length of vectors in this lattice. The caveat is that we can also bound the lengths of the vectors in the*dual*lattice. This crucially allows us to double the density of the admissible instances.\n\nThere are two main differences between our proposal and Howgrave-Graham’s algorithm[[25](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib25)](and the original Coppersmith’s method[[16](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib16)]) Firstly, the number of variables to consider in our case is much larger. Secondly, we only need to construct linear polynomials in these variables rather than considering large degree polynomials.\n\n### 1.2Extension to Block Basis Reduction\n\nWe would like to comment on the possible improvements of using stronger lattice reduction. Specifically, the only aspect of the Lagarias-Odlyzko algorithm that relies on the LLL algorithm is the fact that it provides a(γLLL)n(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}-approximate solution to the Shortest Vector Problem withγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}. Instead of the LLL algorithm, it is natural to apply block-basis reduction (e.g.,[[43](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib43)]) with parameterΥ\\Upsilonarbitrarily close to11. This way, one expects a better approximation of SVP, consequently improving the rangeΓ\\Gamma. The obvious drawback of this is the increased running time.\n\nOur approach, in principle, should allow us to reduce the bitlengths of the Subset Sum instances that can be solved regardless of the concrete algorithm behind the lattice reduction. Unfortunately, adapting our approach to work with every basis lattice reduction requires a separate analysis which is much more technical than for LLL. Nevertheless, the takeaway message is as follows:\n\nThe Modular Arithmetic Approach allows us to solve almost all instances of Subset Sum with integersΩ⁡(Γ)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma})with a single call to lattice reduction, whereas the previous approach guaranteed to solve only instances with integersΩ⁡(Γ)\\Omega(\\Gamma)while using the same lattice reduction algorithm.\n\nSince the LLL algorithm is the most popular we decided first to present our algorithms using this algorithm. In[Section5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S5), we demonstrate that our approach works for the textbook block-reduction presented by Gama and Nguyen[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)].\n\n###### Theorem 1.3.\n\nLetΥ>1\\Upsilon>1be a parameter of lattice reduction in[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)]. Leta1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}be positive integers, selected uniformly at random from range{1,…,⌈Γ⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma}\\right\\rceil\\}, whereΓ=Υn2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma=\\Upsilon^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}. With overwhelming probability, after a single call to a basis reduction algorithm in[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)]with parameterΥ\\Upsilon, we obtain a polynomial time tester, that for every givenT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}decides whetherthere exist​e1,…,en∈{0,1}​such that​∑i=1nei⋅ai=T,\\text{there exist }e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ such that }\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}\\cdot a_{i}=T,\n\nOur tester and its creation method are both deterministic. Their success probability only depends on the random choice of the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}and is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nIn comparison, the standard analysis of Lagarias-Odlyzko algorithm requires the range of integers to be of orderΓ=Υn2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma=\\Upsilon^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}when the lattice reduction with parameterΥ>1\\Upsilon>1is used.\n\n### 1.3Related Work\n\nSubset Sum is an NP-hard problem, closely related to knapsack. The currently fastest algorithm to solve the problem exactly in the worst-case settings runs in𝒪⋆​(2n/2)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{n/2})time[[24](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib24)]. Schroeppel and Shamir[[45](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib45)]show that Subset Sum admits a time-space tradeoff, i.e., an algorithm using𝒮\\mathcal{S}space and2n/𝒮22^{n}/\\mathcal{S}^{2}time for any𝒮⩽𝒪⋆​(2n/4)\\mathcal{S}\\leqslant\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{n/4}). This tradeoff was improved by[[4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib4)]for almost all tradeoff parameters (see also[[18](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib18),[37](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib37)]). In the average-case setting, whend=Θ⁡(1)d=\\Theta(1), Howgrave-Graham and Joux[[26](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib26)]showed𝒪⋆​(20.337​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.337n})time and𝒪⋆​(20.256​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.256n})space algorithm for Subset Sum. This was subsequently improved by Becker, Coron and Joux[[8](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib8)]to𝒪⋆​(20.291​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.291n})time and space.\n\nThe exact running time of the Subset Sum was also analysed in the parameterized setting[[5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib5),[6](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib6)], quantum regime[[3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib3),[23](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib23)], the polynomial space regime[[7](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib7)]. From the perspective of pseudopolynomial algorithms, Subset Sum has also been the subject of recent stimulative research[[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib12),[30](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib30),[1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib1),[40](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib40),[34](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib34)].\n\n## 2Preliminaries\n\nThrough the paper, we let[n]≔{1,…,n}[n]\\coloneqq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}. We use≡p\\equiv_{p}to denote equivalence modulopp, i.e.,a≡pba\\equiv_{p}biff there existsk∈ℤk\\in\\mathbb{Z}such thata=k⋅p+ba=k\\cdot p+b. For integersa,ba,bwe let⟦a⟧b\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{b}be the unique value in set{−⌈b/2⌉+1,…,⌊b/2⌋}\\{-\\left\\lceil b/2\\right\\rceil+1,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor b/2\\right\\rfloor\\}such that⟦a⟧b≡ba\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{b}\\equiv_{b}a.𝒪⋆​(⋅)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(\\cdot)notation hides factors polynomial innn.\n\nWe considernwith the usual Euclidean topology. We use bold letters to denote vectors. The inner product of two vectorsx=(xi)i=1n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n}andy=(yi)i=1n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}=(y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}is denoted by⟨x,y⟩=∑i=1nxi⋅yi\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\\cdot y_{i}. We denote theℓp\\ell_{p}norm as‖x‖p≔(∑i=1n(xi)p)1/p\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert_{p}\\coloneqq(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i})^{p})^{1/p}. Often, we omit the subscriptppwhich means that we consider the Euclidean case withp=2p=2. We denote thedd-dimensional Euclidean ball of radiusRRcentered at0byBalld​(R)\\mathrm{Ball}_{d}(R). We use the Stirling formula to approximate the volume of thedd-dimensional ball, namely:Vol⁡(Balld​(R))∼1d​π​(2​π​ed)d/2⋅Rd=2−Θ⁡(d​log⁡(d))⋅Rd.\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathrm{Ball}_{d}(R))\\sim\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d\\pi}}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi e}{d}\\right)^{d/2}\\cdot R^{d}=2^{-\\Theta(d\\log(d))}\\cdot R^{d}.\n\n### 2.1Background on Lattices\n\nA*lattice*innis a discrete subgroup of(n,+)(\\real^{n},+). This implies that a lattice is a non-empty setL⊆nL\\subseteq\\real^{n}such that for allx,y∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in Lit holds thatx−y∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}-{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in L. Any subgroup of(ℤn,+)(\\mathbb{Z}^{n},+)is a lattice, and it is called an*integral lattice*.\n\nLetb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}be vectors inn. Letℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})be the set of integral combinations of these vectors, namely:ℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)≔{∑i=1mℓi⋅bi​such that​ℓ1,…,ℓm∈ℤ}.\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})\\coloneqq\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\ell_{i}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\text{ such that }\\ell_{1},\\ldots,\\ell_{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}.\n\nThen,ℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})is a lattice, specifically the lattice spanned byb1,…,bm.{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}.Furthermore, when the vectorsb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}are linearly independent (over ), we say that they form a basis ofℒ⁡(b1,…,bm).\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}).In this case, every vectorv∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in Luniquely decomposes as an integral combination of the basis vectors , i.e., for everyv∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in Lthere exists uniqueα1,…,αm∈ℤ\\alpha_{1},\\ldots,\\alpha_{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such thatv=∑i=1mαi⋅bi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\alpha_{i}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}[[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38), Definition 5, p. 26]. It is well-known that every lattice admits basis. The cardinality of any basis is an invariant of the lattice and is called its*dimension*(or sometimes referred to as its rank). This quantity, denoted bydim⁡(L)\\mathrm{dim}(L)is equal to the dimension of its linear span (denotedspan⁡(L)\\mathrm{span}(L)) as a vector space over.\n\nWe say that a latticeL⊆nL\\subseteq\\real^{n}has full rank whenn=dim⁡(L)n=\\mathrm{dim}(L). Since any lattice ofnadmits some basis, we assume w.l.o.g. that every lattice we consider is given in the formℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})for somebi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}’s innwherem=dim⁡(L)m=\\mathrm{dim}(L).\n\nThus,b1,…,bm∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}are linearly independent vectors. Their Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization (GSO) is the orthogonal family(b1∗,…,bm∗)({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{m})defined inductively as follows:\n\nb1∗=b1and\\displaystyle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}\\quad\\text{and}bi∗=bi−∑j=1i−1μi,jbj∗whereμi,j≔⟨bi,bj∗⟩‖bj∗‖2for all1⩽j<i⩽m.\\displaystyle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}-\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}\\mu_{i,j}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\;\\text{ where }\\;\\mu_{i,j}\\coloneqq\\frac{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\rangle}{\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}}\\;\\text{ for all }\\;1\\leqslant j<i\\leqslant m.\n\nWe can define the volume of the latticeLLspanned byb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}asVol⁡(L)=∏i=1m‖bi∗‖,\\mathrm{Vol}(L)=\\prod_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert,\n\nand remark that it is a lattice invariant and does not depend on the choice of basis.\n\nOther classical invariants of a lattice are its successive minima. By definition, theiith successive minimum, denoted asλi​(L)\\lambda_{i}(L)is the smallest real number for which there existiilinearly independent vectors inLLeach of length at mostλi​(L)\\lambda_{i}(L). In particular,λ1​(L)\\lambda_{1}(L)is the length of the shortest nonzero vector inLL. Note thatλ1​(L)⩽λ2​(L)⩽…⩽λm​(L)\\lambda_{1}(L)\\leqslant\\lambda_{2}(L)\\leqslant\\ldots\\leqslant\\lambda_{m}(L).\n\n### 2.2Dual Lattice\n\nFor a latticeL⊂nL\\subset\\real^{n}the*dual*lattice ofLLis defined as:L†≔{y∈span(L)such that⟨x,y⟩∈ℤfor allx∈L}.L^{\\dagger}\\coloneqq\\{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in\\mathrm{span}(L)\\text{ such that }\\langle{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}}\\rangle\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\text{ for all }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in L\\}.\n\nWhenLLis anmm-dimensional lattice ofℝn\\mathbb{R}^{n}thenL†L^{\\dagger}is also anmm-dimensional lattice ofℝn\\mathbb{R}^{n}. More precisely, ifLLis spanned by the rows of the basis matrixBB, thenL†L^{\\dagger}is spanned by the rows of:B†=(B​B⊺)−1​BB^{\\dagger}=\\left(BB^{\\intercal}\\right)^{-1}B\n\nFurthermore, it holds thatVol⁡(L)⋅Vol⁡(L†)=1\\mathrm{Vol}(L)\\cdot\\mathrm{Vol}(L^{\\dagger})=1. Finally, if we reverse the order of the rows ofB†B^{\\dagger}, we obtain the so-called dual basisD†D^{\\dagger}ofBB. The GSO of dual basis is related to the GSO of the primal basis by the following relations:‖bi∗‖⋅‖dm−i+1∗‖=1for every​i∈[m].\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{d}}}_{m-i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert=1\\quad\\mbox{for every}\\ i\\in[m].\n\nIn order to make explicit that the order of the (row) basis vectors is reversed, we write:D†=Rev​((B​B⊺)−1​B).D^{\\dagger}=\\textbf{Rev}\\left(\\left(BB^{\\intercal}\\right)^{-1}B\\right).\n\n### 2.3The LLL algorithm and its property\n\nLenstra, Lenstra and Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]presented a polynomial time procedure that, given an arbitrary basis of a lattice computes a so-called reduced basis with better properties. Extensions of LLL can also compute a reduced basis from an arbitrary generating family.\n\nThe main parameter of LLL is a realδ∈(0.25,1)\\delta\\in(0.25,1). A secondary parameterμ∈[0.5,1)\\mu\\in[0.5,1)is also useful when considering implementations of LLL with finite precision on orthogonalised vectors. The initial LLL with exact rational arithmetic[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]usesμ=1/2\\mu=1/2. Letb1,…,bm∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}be basis of latticeLLandb1∗,…,b∗m∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}be its Gram-Schmidt orthogonalized basis ofLL. We say that(b1,…,bm)({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})is*(δ,μ)(\\delta,\\mu)-LLL reduced*if and only if the following conditions are satisfied:Size Reduction:|μi,k|⩽μ\\displaystyle|\\mu_{i,k}|\\leqslant\\mufor every​1⩽k<i⩽m\\displaystyle\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant k<i\\leqslant mLovász Condition:δ​‖bi∗‖2⩽‖bi+1∗‖2+μi+1,i2​‖bi∗‖2\\displaystyle\\delta\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}\\leqslant\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}+\\mu_{i+1,i}^{2}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}for every​1⩽i<m\\displaystyle\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant i<m\n\nRecall thatμi,k=⟨bi,bk∗⟩⟨bk∗,bk∗⟩\\mu_{i,k}=\\frac{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\rangle}{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\rangle}. Both of these conditions yield an*implied parameter*γLLL≔1δ−μ2\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}\\coloneqq\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\delta-\\mu^{2}}}, such that:\n\n‖bi∗‖⩽γLLL⋅‖bi+1∗‖for every1⩽i<m\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant i<m(1)\n\n[Equation1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)is the strongest forγLLL=4/3+ε\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}=\\sqrt{4/3}+\\varepsilonfor someε>0\\varepsilon>0. This is achieved by takingμ\\musufficiently close to1/21/2andδ\\deltasufficiently close to11. In the sequel, we focus on the parameterγLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}and express our results in terms of it. Note, that the same inequality holds for the dual lattice.\n\nThe running time of LLL algorithm[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]is𝒪​(m5​n⋅polylog​(B))\\mathcal{O}(m^{5}n\\cdot\\text{polylog}(B)), whereBBis the Euclidean length of the longest basis)[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)](see[[42](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib42)]for recent development).\n\n## 3State-of-the-art: Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm\n\nAssume thata=(a1,…,an){\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}=(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})is a vector inℤn\\mathbb{Z}^{n}where eachaia_{i}is chosen independently uniformly at random from{1,…,Γ}\\{1,\\ldots,\\Gamma\\}ande=(e1,…,en)∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}is chosen independently at random. SetT=⟨a,e⟩T=\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\rangle. Then, clearlyeis a solution to the equation:∑i=1nai​xi=T​such that​xi∈{0,1}​for every​i∈{1,…,n}.\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}x_{i}=T\\text{ such that }x_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ for every }i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}.\n\nHere, we recall the argument of Lagarias and Odlyzko who show that ifΓ⩾Ω⁡((γLLL)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma\\geqslant\\Omega((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})})andγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}, then the LLL algorithm is sufficient to solve an instancea1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}with targetTTof Subset Sum in polynomial time and with probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nNote thatT⩽∑i=1naiT\\leqslant\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}, and we can assume that‖e‖1⩽n2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant\\frac{n}{2}as otherwise, we can consider the same instance with target(∑i=1nai−T)\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}-T\\right). LetK≔⌈n​(γLLL)n⌉K\\coloneqq\\left\\lceil n(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\right\\rceilbe a sufficiently large integer. Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]construct the following row-generated lattice:\n\nL≔(10⋯0a1⋅K01⋯0a2⋅K⋱00⋯1an⋅K00⋯0T⋅K).L\\coloneqq\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&\\cdots&0&a_{1}\\cdot K\\\\ 0&1&\\cdots&0&a_{2}\\cdot K\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&\\cdots&1&a_{n}\\cdot K\\\\ 0&0&\\cdots&0&T\\cdot K\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nLete¯=(e1,…,en,0)\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n},0)such that∑i=1nei​ai=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}a_{i}=T. Observe thate¯∈L\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\in Land‖e¯‖⩽n/2\\left\\lVert\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\sqrt{n/2}. Therefore, the vectore¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}is a*short vector*ofLL.\n\nThe idea of Lagarias and Odlyzko is to use the algorithm of Lenstra, Lenstra and Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]. Letvbe a minimum length non-zero vector inLL. The LLL algorithm guarantees finding a nonzero vectorx∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in Lwith:‖x‖⩽(γLLL)n​‖v‖.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\right\\rVert.\n\nHere,γLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}is an implied parameter of the LLL algorithm, and we can guarantee that whenγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}, the LLL algorithm runs in polynomial time (see[Section2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2)). Note that the vectorxfound by the LLL satisfies‖x‖⩽(γLLL)n​‖e¯‖⩽(γLLL)n​n/2<K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\left\\lVert\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\sqrt{n/2}<K\n\nThe strategy is, hence, to run the LLL algorithm and hope that the vectorxreturned by LLL is eithere¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}or−e¯-\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}. There is of course the possibility that there are other spurious vectors of length less thanKK. The analysis by Lagarias and Odlyzko demonstrates that this is, however, highly unlikely. More precisely, they show the following statement:\n\n###### Claim 3.1(cf.,[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]).\n\nℙ𝕣[existsx∈L∖{k⋅e∣k∈ℤ}such that‖x‖<K]⩽(2​K+1)n+1Γ.\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[\\text{exists }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in L\\setminus\\{k\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\mid k\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\}\\text{ such that }\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert<K\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{(2K+1)^{n+1}}{\\Gamma}.\n\nNote that this is2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}whenΓ⩾Ω⁡((γLLL)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma\\geqslant\\Omega((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})}). Therefore, by considering the opposite event, we can guarantee that the vectore¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}is found with a probability at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}. We include the proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)due to[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]as it serves as an introduction to our technique.\n\n###### Proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1).\n\nLetw=(w1,…,wn+1)∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}=(w_{1},\\ldots,w_{n+1})\\in L. Observe that ifwn+1≠0w_{n+1}\\neq 0then already‖w‖⩾K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert\\geqslant K. Hence, it must hold thatwn+1=0w_{n+1}=0. Let𝒲≔{w∈Lsuch that‖w‖<Kandwn+1=0andw≠k⋅e¯for anyk∈ℤ}.\\mathcal{W}\\coloneqq\\{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\in L\\text{ such that }\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert<K\\text{ and }w_{n+1}=0\\text{ and }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\neq k\\cdot\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\text{ for any }k\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\}.\n\nNow, it suffices to bound the probability that𝒲\\mathcal{W}is empty. If, however,𝒲\\mathcal{W}is nonempty, there existsw=(w1,…,wn,0)∈ℤn+1{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}=(w_{1},\\ldots,w_{n},0)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}andℓ∈ℤ\\ell\\in\\mathbb{Z}that satisfy:∑i=1nai⋅wi=ℓ⋅T.\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\\cdot w_{i}=\\ell\\cdot T.(2)\n\nLet us now fix this vectorwand integerℓ\\ell. Letz=(z1,…,zn)∈ℤn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}=(z_{1},\\ldots,z_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n}be such thatzi=wi−ei⋅ℓz_{i}=w_{i}-e_{i}\\cdot\\ell. Observe that⟨a,z⟩=0\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}\\rangle=0. Without loss of generality, we can assume thatz1≠0z_{1}\\neq 0and we letZ≔−(∑i=2nai​zi/z1)Z\\coloneqq-(\\sum_{i=2}^{n}a_{i}z_{i}/z_{1}). Hence,ℙ𝕣[⟨a,z⟩=0]=ℙ𝕣[a1=Z]=∑i=1Γℙ𝕣[a1=i|Z=i]⋅ℙ𝕣[Z=i].\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}\\rangle=0\\right]=\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[a_{1}=Z\\right]=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\Gamma}\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[a_{1}=i\\;|\\;Z=i\\right]\\cdot\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[Z=i\\right].\n\nAsa1a_{1}andZZare independent, this is bounded by∑i=1Γ1Γ⋅ℙ𝕣[Z=i]⩽1Γ.\\sum_{i=1}^{\\Gamma}\\frac{1}{\\Gamma}\\cdot\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[Z=i\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{1}{\\Gamma}.\n\nTherefore, a fixedwandℓ\\ellsatisfy Equation ([2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.E2)) with probability at most1/Γ1/\\Gamma. Note that the number ofw∈ℤn+1{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}andℓ∈ℤ\\ell\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that‖w‖<K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert<K,wn+1=0w_{n+1}=0and|ℓ|⩽2​K|\\ell|\\leqslant 2Kis at most(2​K+1)n+1(2K+1)^{n+1}and the proof concludes. ∎\n\nNote, that the proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)uses lattice reduction as a blackbox. If one were to use lattice reduction with parameterΥ>1\\Upsilon>1, then the range guaranteed by[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)would beΓ=Θ⁡((Υ)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma=\\Theta((\\Upsilon)^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})}). In the next sections, we will show a method to improve the admissible range down toΓ=Θ⁡((Υ)0.5​n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma=\\Theta((\\Upsilon)^{0.5n^{2}+o(n^{2})})that uses more properties of lattice reduction. In[Section4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4)we analyse it with the standard LLL. Then in section[Section5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S5)we analyse it with a textbook block lattice reduction of Gama and Nguyen[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)].\n\n## 4Modular arithmetic approach\n\nIn this section, we focus on the proof of the following statement.\n\nSee[1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)\n\nBefore we do so, let us show how to use[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)to solve the Subset Sum formally.\n\n###### Proof of Theorem[1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)assuming[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\nWe start by describing the algorithm. First, we use[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)to compute the integersμ1,…,μn,p\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n},p. This gives us the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}. We know that this matrix has full rank so we can compute its inverse with the Gaussian elimination algorithm. This concludes the description of the preprocessing phase.\n\nNow, given a targetTTwe compute the vectortp≔(⟦μ1​T⟧p,…,⟦μn​T⟧p)⊺{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}\\coloneqq(\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p})^{\\intercal}and compute a candidate solutione⊺=ℳp−1⋅tp{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}^{\\intercal}=\\mathcal{M}_{p}^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}. Finally, we check thateis indeed correct. Namely, if∑i=1nei​aj=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}a_{j}=Tande∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}we returneand⊥\\bototherwise. This concludes the description of the algorithm. Clearly, the algorithm runs in polynomial time. Moreover, a query takes only𝒪⁡(n)\\mathcal{O}(n)arithmetic operations (on numbers from the range[R][R]). The success probability of the algorithm comes exclusively from a single application of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). Therefore, it remains to argue about the correctness.\n\nLete1,…,en∈{0,1}ne_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}be a solution to∑i=1nai​ei=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}e_{i}=Tand assume that our algorithm returns⊥\\bot. Because the norm‖⟦μi​a⟧p‖1\\left\\lVert\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}\\right\\rVert_{1}is bounded byp/2p/2it holds that:\n\n(⟦μ1​a1⟧p⟦μ1​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ1​an⟧p⟦μ2​a1⟧p⟦μ2​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ2​an⟧p⟦μn​a1⟧p⟦μn​a2⟧p⋯⟦μn​an⟧p)​(e1e2en)=(⟦μ1​T⟧p⟦μ2​T⟧p⟦μn​T⟧p)\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots&&&\\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}e_{1}\\\\ e_{2}\\\\ \\vdots\\\\ e_{n}\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\n\nSince the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full rank, there exists a unique solution to the above linear system. Hence(e1,…,en)⊺=ℳp−1⋅tp(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})^{\\intercal}=\\mathcal{M}_{p}^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}. Finally, note that we only return this solution if all its coordinates are in{0,1}n\\{0,1\\}^{n}, thus ensuring an incorrect solution is never returned. ∎\n\nFrom now on we will focus on the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\n### 4.1Generating Family\n\nLetp∈ℕp\\in\\mathbb{N}be the prime fixed in[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). In particular,ppis odd. Because the numbersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}are generated at random in the interval{1,…,ΓLO}\\{1,\\ldots,\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\}which has length much larger thanpp, the modular reductionsai(modp)a_{i}\\pmod{p}are very close to uniform modulopp. For simplicity of the analysis we want to assume that they are uniform. This can be achieved by using the rejection sampling to discard any event when at least oneaia_{i}is greater thanσp\\sigma_{p}, whereσp\\sigma_{p}is the largest multiple ofppsuch thatσp<ΓLO\\sigma_{p}<\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}. This is valid, because the probability that specificaia_{i}is discarded is:\n\nℙ[ai>σp]⩽|ΓLO−σp|ΓLO⩽2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n).\\mathbb{P}\\left[a_{i}>\\sigma_{p}\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{|\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}-\\sigma_{p}|}{\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}}\\leqslant 2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nTherefore, by the union bound, probability that we do not discard any ofa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}. Similarly, we can assume thata1a_{1}is not a multiple ofpp. Hence, there exists an integera1−1a_{1}^{-1}such thata1⋅a1−1≡p1a_{1}\\cdot a_{1}^{-1}\\equiv_{p}1.\n\nConsider the lattice generated by the rows of the following matrix:ℒ=(a1a2a3⋯anp00⋯00p0000p0⋱000…p)∈ℤ(n+1)×n\\mathcal{L}=\\begin{pmatrix}a_{1}&a_{2}&a_{3}&\\cdots&a_{n}\\\\ p&0&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ 0&p&0&&0\\\\ 0&0&p&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&0&\\ldots&p\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{(n+1)\\times n}(3)\n\nNow, let us elaborate on the connection between matrixℒ\\mathcal{L}and any vector(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})from the statement of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\n###### Observation 4.1.\n\nFor every integerμ∈ℤ\\mu\\in\\mathbb{Z}it holds that:\n\n(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)∈ℒ.(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})\\in\\mathcal{L}.\n\nConversely, for every vectorv∈ℒ∩{−⌊p/2⌋,…,⌊p/2⌋}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in\\mathcal{L}\\cap\\{-\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor\\}^{n}, there existsμ′∈ℤ\\mu^{\\prime}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that:v=(⟦μ′​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ′​an⟧p).{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=(\\llbracket\\mu^{\\prime}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu^{\\prime}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}).\n\nMoreover,μ′\\mu^{\\prime}is unique moduloppand its exact value can be determined with a constant number of arithmetic operations.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nFor the first property observe that triviallyμ⋅(a1,…,an)∈ℒ\\mu\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})\\in\\mathcal{L}. Letei{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}be the vector with11at theiith coordinate and00at the remaining coordinates. Note that by definition(p⋅ei)∈ℒ(p\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i})\\in\\mathcal{L}for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. Then we construct the desired vector by\n\n(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)=μ⋅(a1,…,an)−∑i=1n⌊μ​aip⌋⋅ei∈ℒ.(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})=\\mu\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\mu a_{i}}{p}\\right\\rfloor\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}\\in\\mathcal{L}.\n\nFor the converse property, by definition any vectorv∈ℒ{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in\\mathcal{L}is represented as:\n\nv=k0⋅(a1,…,an)+∑i=1n(ki​p)⋅ei.{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=k_{0}\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(k_{i}p)\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}.\n\nfor some integersk0,…,kn∈ℤk_{0},\\ldots,k_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}. Let us inspect theiith coordinate ofv. For everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]it holds thatk0⋅ai+ki⋅p∈{−⌊p/2⌋,…,⌊p/2⌋}k_{0}\\cdot a_{i}+k_{i}\\cdot p\\in\\{-\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor\\}. Therefore, for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n], theiith coordinate ofvis⟦k0​ai⟧p\\llbracket k_{0}a_{i}\\rrbracket_{p}. We hence can setμ′≔k0\\mu^{\\prime}\\coloneqq k_{0}. Note, that given vectorv, the integerμ′\\mu^{\\prime}can be computed efficiently because it is expressed asμ′=v1⋅a1−1\\mu^{\\prime}=v_{1}\\cdot a_{1}^{-1}, wherev1v_{1}is the first coordinate ofv. ∎\n\nNow, let us further elaborate on the subsequent steps. We run the LLL algorithm onℒ\\mathcal{L}. The LLL algorithm returns a basisb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}ofℒ\\mathcal{L}. We show that, with high probability,‖bi‖1⩽p/2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant p/2for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. If that occurs we are nearly finished. By the[4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem1)each of the basis vectorsbi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}is equal to(⟦μi​a1⟧p,…,⟦μi​an⟧p)(\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})for some integerμi∈ℤ\\mu_{i}\\in\\mathbb{Z}. Moreover, because the vectorsb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}form a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}it means that the matrix formed by the vectorsb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\dots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}has a full-rank.\n\nTherefore, to complete the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)it suffices to show that‖bi‖1⩽p/2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant p/2for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. By Cauchy-Schwartz inequality it is actually sufficient to prove that‖bi‖2⩽p2​n\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{n}}.\n\n### 4.2Basis of Generating Family and Dual Basis\n\nLatticeℒ\\mathcal{L}is given to us by a generating family in form of a rectangular matrix. It is much more convenient to work with a basis, especially when given by a square matrice. Therefore, we start by determining a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}. As we have already noticed, we can assume without loss of generality thata1a_{1}is invertible inℤp\\mathbb{Z}_{p}. For everyi∈2,…,ni\\in{2,\\ldots,n}, letαi=ai​a1−1\\alpha_{i}=a_{i}a^{-1}_{1}. The rows of the following matrix form a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}:\n\nℬ0=(1α2α3⋯αn0p0000p0⋱000…p)∈ℤn×n\\mathcal{B}_{0}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\alpha_{2}&\\alpha_{3}&\\cdots&\\alpha_{n}\\\\ 0&p&0&&0\\\\ 0&0&p&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&0&\\ldots&p\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n\\times n}\n\nIn particular, this implies that the volume ofℒ\\mathcal{L}ispn−1p^{n-1}. We can determine the dual lattice ofℒ\\mathcal{L}, which in the full-dimensional case is spanned by the rows of the transpose of the inverse matrix. In our case, this is:ℬ0†=(ℬ0−1)T=1p⋅(p00⋯0−α210⋯0−α3010⋱−αn00…1)∈ℚn×n\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{\\dagger}=(\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{-1})^{T}=\\frac{1}{p}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix}p&0&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ -\\alpha_{2}&1&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ -\\alpha_{3}&0&1&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ -\\alpha_{n}&0&0&\\ldots&1\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Q}^{n\\times n}\n\nObserve thatp​ℬ0†p\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{\\dagger}generates the set of integral vectors(x1,…,xn)∈ℤn(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n}such that∑i=2nxi⋅αi≡p−x1\\sum_{i=2}^{n}x_{i}\\cdot\\alpha_{i}\\equiv_{p}-x_{1}. Or equivalently such that:∑i=1nxiai≡p0.\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}a_{i}\\equiv_{p}0.\n\n### 4.3Every vector of the LLL-reduced basis is probably short\n\nNow, letℬ\\mathcal{B}be an LLL-reduced basis of the latticeℒ\\mathcal{L}. We denote its row vectors byb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}and their GSO byb1∗,…,bn∗{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}. Recall that the dual basis, given byℬ†=Rev​((ℬ−1)T)\\mathcal{B}^{\\dagger}=\\textbf{Rev}((\\mathcal{B}^{-1})^{T}).\n\nIn this section, we prove the following probabilistic property on a reduced basis:\n\n###### Lemma 4.2.\n\nWith probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}, it holds that:‖bk∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−12​Vol​(ℒ)1/nfor every​k∈{1,…,n}.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}\\quad\\text{for every}\\ k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}.\n\n[Lemma4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem2)can be proved as a consequence of the following inequalities, which hold with overwhelming probability when the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}are drawn uniformly at random.\n\n###### Claim 4.3.\n\nWith probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}it holds that:‖bn∗‖⩽Vol​(ℒ)1/n⩽‖b1∗‖.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}\\leqslant\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert.\n\n###### Proof of[Lemma4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem2)assuming[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3).\n\nLet us assume that both inequalities in[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3)hold. Ifk⩾n+12k\\geqslant\\frac{n+1}{2}then by repeated application of Inequality ([1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)) we have that‖bk∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−k​‖bn∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−12​Vol​(ℒ)1/n\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n-k}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}, which is the desired result. Hence we need to focus on the case wherek<n+12k<\\frac{n+1}{2}. Observe that by repeated application of Inequality ([1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)) and[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3)we have‖b1∗‖⋯‖bk−1∗‖\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdots\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k-1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert⩾∏i=1k−1‖b1∗‖(γLLL)i−1=(γLLL)−(k−1)​(k−2)2⋅‖b1∗‖k−1⩾(γLLL)−(k−1)​(k−2)2⋅Vol​(ℒ)k−1n.\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\prod_{i=1}^{k-1}\\frac{\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{i-1}}=(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}{2}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{k-1}\\geqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}{2}}\\cdot\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{\\frac{k-1}{n}}.Similarly, we have‖bk∗‖⋯‖bn∗‖\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdots\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert⩾∏i=0n−k‖bk∗‖(γLLL)i=(γLLL)−(n−k)​(n−k+1)2⋅‖bk∗‖n−k+1.\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\prod_{i=0}^{n-k}\\frac{\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{i}}=(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{n-k+1}.Recall that the volume of the latticeℒ\\mathcal{L}isVol(ℒ)=‖b1∗‖⋯‖bn∗‖\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})=\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdots\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert. Hence by multiplying the above inequalities we haveVol⁡(ℒ)\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})⩾Vol​(ℒ)k−1n⋅‖bk∗‖n−k+1⋅(γLLL)−(n−k)​(n−k+1)2−(k−1)​(k−2)2.\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{\\frac{k-1}{n}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{n-k+1}\\cdot(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}{2}}.Becausek<n+12k<\\frac{n+1}{2}we have that(n−k)​(n−k+1)+(k−1)​(k−2)⩽(n−1)​(n−k+1)(n-k)(n-k+1)+(k-1)(k-2)\\leqslant(n-1)(n-k+1). HenceVol⁡(ℒ)\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})⩾Vol​(ℒ)k−1n⋅‖bk∗‖n−k+1⋅(γLLL)−(n−1)​(n−k+1)2.\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{\\frac{k-1}{n}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{n-k+1}\\cdot(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(n-1)(n-k+1)}{2}}.\n\nBy rearranging the terms we haveVol​(ℒ)n−k+1n⋅(γLLL)(n−1)​(n−", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:39.071382+00:00"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"question": "There are infinite prime numbers—numbers that are only divisible by one and the number itself. Their existence and properties are extremely interesting to mathematicians, computer scientists, and other experts. While the entire number line—essentially every number—can be expressed as the product of prime numbers, there is great difficulty in factoring large numbers into primes. And since prime numbers have these unique properties associated with factorization, they are very useful in the field of cryptography. Imagine a computer encryption that relies on an extremely large number, such as a number with many factors of tens or even hundreds and hundreds of digits; even supercomputers will have a huge challenge in identifying its prime factors, making prime numbers especially appealing for encryption.", "direction": {"title": "Computational asymmetry between primality testing and integer factorization", "rationale": "Synthesize theoretical computer science literature to clarify why deterministic primality verification resides in P while exact factorization remains computationally intractable.", "workflow": "evidence_synthesis", "completion_test": "A structured review explicitly maps algorithmic classes, cites peer-reviewed complexity proofs, and distinguishes worst-case from average-case hardness.", "remaining": "Engineering teams must choose which hardness assumptions align with current hardware capabilities and threat models for deployment.", "position": 2}}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"queries": ["deterministic primality testing polynomial time AKS proof versus integer factorization computational intractability", "integer factorization worst-case average-case hardness algorithmic classes cryptographic assumptions"], "sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:8bc5295a2d4242bcc2624f816ea29b0d3f7d56401a62167c588ee6bc5ddaafd6", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "Why is not known whether integer factorization can be done in polynomial time knowing how to do primality tests efficiently?", "url": "https://cs.stackexchange.com/questions/50767/why-is-not-known-whether-integer-factorization-can-be-done-in-polynomial-time-kn", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:1c996ca287c8403a9c5e68f103999611dd6f5829546e45255636a3f087f180e6", "artifact_id": "artifact:1403795b45a08ab6e3a7983ba5c5b5bd4a8ffdb6026d6583a760b19a7d8a9c06", "sha256": "1403795b45a08ab6e3a7983ba5c5b5bd4a8ffdb6026d6583a760b19a7d8a9c06", "title": "AKS primality test", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 341}, "content": "## AKS primality test - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nAlgorithm checking for prime numbers\n\nThe**AKS primality test**(also known as the**Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena primality test**and the**cyclotomic AKS test**) is\na[deterministic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)[primality-\nproving](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_test)[algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithm)created and published by[Manindra\nAgrawal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manindra_Agrawal),[Neeraj Kayal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neeraj_Kayal), and[Nitin\nSaxena](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitin_Saxena), computer scientists at the[Indian Institute of Technology\nKanpur](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Institute_of_Technology_Kanpur), on August 6, 2002, in an article titled \"PRIMES is in\nP\".[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)The algorithm was the first one which is able to determine in[polynomial\ntime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time), whether a given number\nis[prime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number)or[composite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composite_number)without relying on[mathematical\nconjectures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_conjecture)such as the[generalized Riemann\nhypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis). The proof is also notable for not relying on the field\nof[analysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analysis_(mathematics)).[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-2)In 2006 the authors\nreceived both the[Gödel Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del_Prize)and[Fulkerson Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulkerson_Prize)for their work.\n\n## Importance[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nAKS is the first primality-proving algorithm to be simultaneously*general*,*polynomial-time*,*deterministic*, and*unconditionally correct*. Previous algorithms\nhad been developed for centuries and achieved three of these properties at most, but not all four.\n- The AKS algorithm can be used to verify the primality of any**general**number given. Many fast primality tests are known that work only for numbers with\ncertain properties. For example, the[Lucas–Lehmer test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas%E2%80%93Lehmer_primality_test)works only for[Mersenne\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mersenne_number), while[Pépin's test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%A9pin%27s_test)can be applied to[Fermat\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number)only.\n- The maximum running time of the algorithm can be bounded by a**[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time#Polynomial_time)**over the number of\ndigits in the target number.[ECPP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve_primality_proving)and[APR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adleman%E2%80%93Pomeran\nce%E2%80%93Rumely_primality_test)conclusively prove or disprove that a given number is prime, but are not known to have polynomial time bounds for all inputs.\n- The algorithm is guaranteed to distinguish**[deterministically](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)**whether the target number is prime or\ncomposite. Randomized tests, such as[Miller–Rabin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test)and[Baillie–PSW](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\niki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test), can test any given number for primality in polynomial time, but are known to produce only a probabilistic result.\n- The correctness of AKS is**not conditional**on any subsidiary unproved[hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothesis). In contrast, Miller's version of\nthe[Miller–Rabin test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test#Deterministic_variants)is fully deterministic and runs in polynomial\ntime over all inputs, but its correctness depends on the truth of the yet-unproved[generalized Riemann\nhypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis).\n\nWhile the algorithm is of immense theoretical importance, it is not used in practice, rendering it a[galactic\nalgorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_algorithm). For 64-bit inputs, the[Baillie–PSW\ntest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test)is deterministic and runs many orders of magnitude faster. For larger inputs, the\nperformance of the (also unconditionally correct) ECPP and APR tests is*far*superior to AKS. Additionally, ECPP can output a[primality\ncertificate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_certificate)that allows independent and rapid verification of the results, which is not possible with the\nAKS algorithm.\n\n## Concepts[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=2)]\n\nThe AKS primality test is based upon the following theorem: Given an integern≥2{\\displaystyle n\\geq 2}and integera{\\displaystyle\na}[coprime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime)ton{\\displaystyle n},n{\\displaystyle n}is prime if and only if\nthe[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)[congruence\nrelation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congruence_relation#Modular_arithmetic)(X+a)n≡Xn+a(modn){\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}\\equiv X^{n}+a{\\pmod {n}}}1\n\nholds within the polynomial ring(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-\nAKS-1)Note thatX{\\displaystyle X}denotes the[indeterminate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring#Definition_(univariate_case))which generates this\npolynomial ring.\n\nThis theorem is a generalization to polynomials of[Fermat's little theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat's_little_theorem). In one direction it can\neasily be proved using the[binomial theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem)together with the following property of the[binomial\ncoefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient):(nk)≡0(modn){\\displaystyle {n \\choose k}\\equiv 0{\\pmod {n}}}for all0<k<n{\\displaystyle\n0<k<n}ifn{\\displaystyle n}is prime.\n\nWhile the relation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_1)**) constitutes a primality test in itself, verifying it takes[exponential\ntime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_time): the[brute force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute_force_method)approach would require the expansion of\nthe(X+a)n{\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}}polynomial and a reduction(modn){\\displaystyle {\\pmod {n}}}of the resultingn+1{\\displaystyle n+1}coefficients.\n\nThe congruence is an equality in the[polynomial ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}.\nEvaluating in a quotient ring of(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}creates an upper bound for the degree of the polynomials involved. The\nAKS evaluates the equality in(Z/nZ)[X]/(Xr−1){\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]/(X^{r}-1)}, making the[computational\ncomplexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_complexity_theory)dependent on the size ofr{\\displaystyle r}. For\nclarity,[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)this is expressed as the congruence(X+a)n≡(Xn+a)(modXr−1,n){\\displaystyle\n(X+a)^{n}\\equiv (X^{n}+a){\\pmod {X^{r}-1,n}}}2\n\nwhich is the same as:(X+a)n−(Xn+a)=(Xr−1)g+nf{\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}-(X^{n}+a)=(X^{r}-1)g+nf}3"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:0f69af372ba3c221eed6e469885a0b96b8901ba513c8935cd11bd6b72d8b6c4e", "artifact_id": "artifact:e6d74098c2706366bcc343ac26fee7cbaa111844e0642556bc42327d03189e7f", "sha256": "e6d74098c2706366bcc343ac26fee7cbaa111844e0642556bc42327d03189e7f", "title": "Primality Testing in Polynomial Time: From Randomized Algorithms to \"PRIMES Is in P\" | Springer Nature Link", "url": "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 8}, "content": "## Client Challenge\n\n**Source**: https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334\n\n---\n\nA required part of this site couldn’t load. This may be due to a browser extension, network issues, or browser settings. Please check your connection, disable\nany ad blockers, or try using a different browser."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:a4961ccca36b97c4722a89f5ee273d468a89d9f74e225a8a67cd42e1526ea03a", "artifact_id": "artifact:5b76592e66de71aaf6613906161aa86db5bd641a862e8ecdb21efe5035d6df5b", "sha256": "5b76592e66de71aaf6613906161aa86db5bd641a862e8ecdb21efe5035d6df5b", "title": "Identifying primes in polynomial time: the AKS algorithm", "url": "https://academicweb.nd.edu/~andyp/notes/AKS.pdf", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_unsupported_content\nResponse content type is not supported."}]}, "tool_receipts": [{"id": "tool-receipt:03ae873067421d7db88433f2", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "search", "arguments": {"query": "deterministic primality testing polynomial time AKS proof versus integer factorization computational intractability", "max_results": 5}, "result": "## Search Results (5 results, 289ms)\n\n### 1. Why is not known whether integer factorization can be done in polynomial time knowing how to do primality tests efficiently?\n- **URL**: https://cs.stackexchange.com/questions/50767/why-is-not-known-whether-integer-factorization-can-be-done-in-polynomial-time-kn\n- # Why is not known whether integer factorization can be done in polynomial time knowing how to do primality tests efficiently? Tags: algorithms, complexity-theory, time-complexity, factoring - Score: 8 - Views: 4076 - Answers: 5 - Answered: yes - Asked by: calm-tedesco (191 rep) - Asked: 2015-12-15 - Edited: 2015-12-15 - Site: cs ## Question First of all, I have just started studying computer science by myself and maybe I just need some clarification of what \"polynomial time\" means regarding the time complexity of an algorithm and references to study it well. As I have understood it, whether integer factorization can be done in polynomial time is still an open question and, as this article in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_factorization) puts it, When the numbers are very large, no efficient, non-quantum integer factorization algorithm is known; an effort by several researchers concluded in 2009, factoring a 232-digit number (RSA-768), utilizing hundreds of machines took two years and the researchers estimated that a 1024-bit RSA modulus would take about a thousand times as long. So, trying to see that for myself, I have written a very naive code in MATLAB checking it with prime numbers up to 15 digits; the reasoning being that if I can check if a number is prime fast, I can easily modify the code to give me the factorization fast. The time it takes the code to check if a number is prime doesn't grow exponentially with the input. function[]=prime(n) tic f=floor(sqrt(n)); for i=2:f if rem(n,i)~=0 b=0; else b=1; disp(i) break end end if b==0 disp('prime') else disp('not prime') end toc end And so I go back to the question in the title. What is wrong with my reasoning? ## Answers ### Answer by Tom van der Zanden (score: 33 [ACCEPTED]) Since your algorithm is \"fast\", why did you only try it with a 15-digit number and not with a 232-digit one? There's serious money to be made if you indeed have a \"fast\" algorithm. Your algorithm takes time (if we count \"div\" as taking constant time) proportional to $\\sqrt{n}$. A $d$-digit number can be as large as $10^d$, so your algorithm takes time proportional to $\\sqrt{10^d} \\approx 3.16^d$, i.e. exponential in $d$, the number of digits. That is by no means \"fast\" and grows very quickly as the numbers get larger. It is polynomial with respect to the value of $n$, but not with respect to the size of $n$. This behavior is called pseudopolynomiality. The \"fast\" prime testing algorithms use much more sophisticated approaches which can not be modified (easily) to also give a factorization. They just report yes/no whether the number is prime. The AKS primality test uses time proportional to $d^6$. ### Answer by Hendrik Jan (score: 13) The point is that the complexity of an algorithms is measured in the size of the input. For a number $n$ its size, the length of its representation, is $\\log n$ bits. ### Answer by Kaveh (score: 3) There can be different algorithms to solve a problem and there is no reason that all of them should follow some particular structure. It is a common mistake to assume that to check if a number is not a prime an algorithm needs to find a non-trivial factor of the number. This is not true. The AKS algorithm does not find any factors when it reports a number is not prime. AKS is rather complicated so let's look at the simpler Miller–Rabin algorithm (ignoring the fact that it is a probabilistic algorithm). Let $n$ be the number we want to check if it is prime or not. The case of even numbers is easy so we consider the case of odd $n$. Let $n-1 = d2^s$ where $d$ is an odd number. The idea is: $n$ is not prime iff for all $0 < a < n$ and $0 \\leq r < s$, $$a^d \\not\\equiv 1 \\ (\\text{mod } n)$$ and $$a^{2^rd} \\not\\equiv -1 \\ (\\text{mod } n)$$ Assume that we checked these conditions, then we know $n$ is not a prime without finding a nontrivial factor of $n$. ### Answer by gnasher729 (score: 1) When a know fast primali\n\n### 2. AKS primality test\n- **URL**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test\n- AKS primality test The AKS primality test (also known as the Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena primality test and the cyclotomic AKS test) is a deterministic primality-proving algorithm created and published by Manindra Agrawal, Neeraj Kayal, and Nitin Saxena, computer scientists at the Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, on August 6, 2002, in an article titled \"PRIMES is in P\". [1] The algorithm was the first one which is able to determine in polynomial time, whether a given number is prime or composite without relying on mathematical conjectures such as the generalized Riemann hypothesis. The proof is also notable for not relying on the field of analysis. [2] In 2006 the authors received both the Gödel Prize and Fulkerson Prize for their work. ## Importance AKS is the first primality-proving algorithm to be simultaneously general, polynomial-time, deterministic, and unconditionally correct. Previous algorithms had been developed for centuries and achieved three of these properties at most, but not all four. - The AKS algorithm can be used to verify the primality of any general number given. Many fast primality tests are known that work only for numbers with certain properties. For example, the Lucas–Lehmer test works only for Mersenne numbers, while Pépin's test can be applied to Fermat numbers only. - The maximum running time of the algorithm can be bounded by a polynomial over the number of digits in the target number. ECPP and APR conclusively prove or disprove that a given number is prime, but are not known to have polynomial time bounds for all inputs. - The algorithm is guaranteed to distinguish deterministically whether the target number is prime or composite. Randomized tests, such as Miller–Rabin and Baillie–PSW, can test any given number for primality in polynomial time, but are known to produce only a probabilistic result. - The correctness of AKS is not conditional on any subsidiary unproved hypothesis. In contrast, Miller's version of the Miller–Rabin test is fully deterministic and runs in polynomial time over all inputs, but its correctness depends on the truth of the yet-unproved generalized Riemann hypothesis. While the algorithm is of immense theoretical importance, it is not used in practice, rendering it a galactic algorithm. For 64-bit inputs, the Baillie–PSW test is deterministic and runs many orders of magnitude faster. For larger inputs, the performance of the (also unconditionally correct) ECPP and APR tests is far superior to AKS. Additionally, ECPP can output a primality certificate that allows independent and rapid verification of the results, which is not possible with the AKS algorithm. ## Concepts The AKS primality test is based upon the following theorem: Given an integer and integer coprime to , is prime if and only if the polynomial congruence relation $ ( X + a ) n ≡ X n + a ( mod n ) {\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}\\equiv X^{n}+a{\\pmod {n}}} $ 1 holds within the polynomial ring $ ( Z / n Z ) [ X ] {\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]} $. [1] Note that denotes the indeterminate which generates this polynomial ring. This theorem is a generalization to polynomials of Fermat's little theorem. In one direction it can easily be proved using the binomial theorem together with the following property of the binomial coefficient: - $ ( n k ) ≡ 0 ( mod n ) {\\displaystyle {n \\choose k}\\equiv 0{\\pmod {n}}} $ for all if is prime. While the relation (1) constitutes a primality test in itself, verifying it takes exponential time: the brute force approach would require the expansion of the $ ( X + a ) n {\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}} $ polynomial and a reduction $ ( mod n ) {\\displaystyle {\\pmod {n}}} $ of the resulting coefficients. The congruence is an equality in the polynomial ring $ ( Z / n Z\n\n### 3. Primality Testing in Polynomial Time: From Randomized Algorithms to \"PRIMES Is in P\" | Springer Nature Link\n- **URL**: https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334\n- Primality Testing in Polynomial Time: From Randomized Algorithms to \"PRIMES Is in P\" | Springer Nature Link ## Overview Authors: - Martin Dietzfelbinger 0 Martin Dietzfelbinger Technische Universität Ilmenau, Ilmenau, Germany Search author on: PubMed Google Scholar - Describes the new deterministic polynomial time primality test (Agrawal/Kayal/Saxena) with complete analysis in a consolidated way - Includes supplementary material: sn.pub/extras Part of the book series: Lecture Notes in Computer Science(LNCS, volume 3000) 11k Accesses 19 Citations ## Access this book ## Other ways to access Licence this eBook for your library ## About this book On August 6, 2002,a paper with the title “PRIMES is in P”, by M. Agrawal, N. Kayal, and N. Saxena, appeared on the website of the Indian Institute of Technology at Kanpur, India. In this paper it was shown that the “primality problem”hasa“deterministic algorithm” that runs in “polynomial time”. Finding out whether a given number n is a prime or not is a problem that was formulated in ancient times, and has caught the interest of mathema- ciansagainandagainfor centuries. Onlyinthe 20thcentury,with theadvent of cryptographic systems that actually used large prime numbers, did it turn out to be of practical importance to be able to distinguish prime numbers and composite numbers of signi?cant size. Readily, algorithms were provided that solved the problem very e?ciently and satisfactorily for all practical purposes, and provably enjoyed a time bound polynomial in the number of digits needed to write down the input number n. The only drawback of these algorithms is that they use “randomization” — that means the computer that carries out the algorithm performs random experiments, and there is a slight chance that the outcome might be wrong, or that the running time might not be polynomial. To ?nd an algorithmthat gets by without rand- ness, solves the problem error-free, and has polynomial running time had been an eminent open problem in complexity theory for decades when the paper by Agrawal, Kayal, and Saxena hit the web. ### Limiting Check Algorithm: Primality Test ### Design and Implementation of the Primitive Polynomial Search System in the Prime Number Field ### Functional Pearl: Folding Polynomials of Polynomials Search within this book Search ### Front Matter ### 1. Introduction: Efficient Primality Testing - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 1-12 ### 2. Algorithms for Numbers and Their Complexity - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 13-21 ### 3. Fundamentals from Number Theory - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 23-53 ### 4. Basics from Algebra: Groups, Rings, and Fields - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 55-71 ### 5. The Miller-Rabin Test - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 73-84 ### 6. The Solovay-Strassen Test - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 85-94 ### 7. More Algebra: Polynomials and Fields - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 95-114 ### 8. Deterministic Primality Testing in Polynomial Time - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 115-131 ### A. Appendix - Martin Dietzfelbinger Pages 133-142 ### Back Matter Back to top ## Reviews From the reviews: \"This book gives an account of the recent proof by M. Agrawal, N. Kayal and N. Saxena … that one can decide in polynomial time whether a given natural number is prime or composite. … It presents the background needed from number theory and algebra to make the proof accessible to undergraduates. … This concise book is written for students of computer science and of mathematics.\" (Samuel S. Wagstaff, Mathematical Reviews, Issue 2005 m) \"The book can logically be separated into two parts: the first covering introductory material and the second covering the AKS result itself. … Chapters … are a joy to read, and I found the proofs and explanations clear and concise. Amazingly, the material is presented in full, with complete proofs given for all results necessary for proving the main results of the book. … I would enthusiastically and whol\n\n### 4. Identifying primes in polynomial time: the AKS algorithm\n- **URL**: https://academicweb.nd.edu/~andyp/notes/AKS.pdf\n- #### Identifying primes in polynomial time: the AKS algorithm Andrew Putman Abstract We prove a remarkable theorem Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena saying that there is an algorithm to determine if an integer is prime whose running time is a polynomial in the number of digits of the integer. ##### 1 Introduction Given an integer n ≥ 2, the most naive way to determine if n is prime is to test whether or not n is divisible by m for all 2 ≤ m ≤ ⌊√n⌋ . This has computational complexity √n; however, it is natural to want a algorithm whose computational complexity is not a polynomial in n itself, but rather in the number of digits of n, i.e. a polynomial in log n. A remarkable theorem of Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena says that such an algorithm exists: Theorem 1.1 ([1, 2]). There exists an algorithm for determining if an integer n ≥ 2 is prime with computational complexity log C n for some constant C. The goal of this note is to prove Theorem 1.1. Remarkably, the proof is almost entirely elementary. Remark 1.2. Rather than summarizing the history leading up to Theorem 1.1, we will simply refer the reader to the introduction of [1] or to the detailed survey [3]. The outline of this note is as follows. In §2, we state Theorem 2.2, which is the characterization of prime numbers underlying Theorem 1.1. We then show how to derive Theorem 1.1 from Theorem 2.2. The proof of Theorem 2.2 requires a weak form of the prime number theorem that we prove in §3. In §4, we extract from our weak form of the prime number theorem the precise statement we need. Finally, in §5, we reduce Theorem 2.2 to a statement about the arithmetic of Fp and in §6 we prove this arithmetic statement. Throughout these notes, log(n) denotes the base-2 logarithm of n and ϕ(r) denotes the Euler totient function. Acknowledgments. I would like to thank Peter Shalen for some helpful corrections. ##### 2 The algorithm If n is prime, then in (Z/n)[x] it is easy to see that (x + a) n ≡ x n + a for all a ∈ Z/n. The converse to this is also true; indeed, something even stronger holds: Lemma 2.1. Consider some n ≥ 2. Assume that for some a ∈ Z with gcd(a, n) = 1 we have (x + a) n ≡ x n + a in (Z/n)[x]. Then n is prime. Proof. Assume that n is not prime, and let p be a prime dividing n. If p m is the maximal power of p dividing n, then n p = n · (n − 1)· · · (n − p + 1) p · (p − 1)· · · (1) is not divisible by p m, so the term n p x pa n− p of the binomial expansion of (x + a) n does not vanish in (Z/n)[x]. To test if some n ≥ 2 is prime, therefore, it would be enough to prove that (x + 1) n ≡ x n + 1 in (Z/n)[x]. Unfortunately, (x + 1) n has (n + 1) terms, so this algorithm would have computational complexity a polynomial in n. The main theorem underlying Theorem 1.1 avoids this issue by expanding binomials not in (Z/n)[x], but rather in (Z/n)[x]/(x r − 1) for some appropriate r: Theorem 2.2. Consider some n ≥ 2. For all 1 ≤ r ≤ ⌈ log 5 n⌉ and 1 ≤ a ≤ ⌊p ϕ(r) log n⌋ , assume that the following hold: • gcd(a, n) = 1, and • In (Z/n)[x]/(x r − 1), we have (x + a) n ≡ x n + a. Then n is a power of a prime. The remainder of this section shows how to prove Theorem 1.1 from Theorem 2.2. The following sections then prove Theorem 2.2. We start by observing that the hypotheses of Theorem 2.2 can only be satisfied for fairly large n. Indeed, since gcd(n, n) ̸ = 1, for the hypotheses of Theorem 2.2 to hold we must have that a = n does not satisfy the inequalities in its statement. The following lemma shows that assuming that n ≥ 10000 ensures this: Lemma 2.3. Consider some n ≥ 10000. Then for all 1 ≤ r ≤ ⌈ log 5 n⌉ and 1 ≤ a ≤ ⌊p ϕ(r) log n⌋ , we have a < n. Proof. Using the trivial inequality ϕ(r) ≤ r − 1, we have a ≤q ϕ(log 5(n) + 1) log n ≤q log 5 n log n = log 3.5 n. It is an easy exercise to see that log 3.5 n < n for n ≥ 10000. We now prove Theorem 1.1. Proof of Theorem 1.1, assuming Theorem 2.2. Consider some integer n ≥ 2. We determine if n is prime via the fo\n\n### 5. Deterministic Polynomial Time Equivalence of Computing the RSA Secret Key and Factoring\n- **URL**: https://eprint.iacr.org/2004/208.pdf\n- ### Deterministic Polynomial Time Equivalence of Computing the RSA Secret Key and Factoring Jean-S´ebastien Coron and Alexander May Gemplus Card International 34 rue Guynemer, 92447 Issy-les-Moulineaux, France jean-sebastien.coron@gemplus.com Faculty of Computer Science, Electrical Engineering and Mathematics University of Paderborn 33102 Paderborn, Germany alexx@uni-paderborn.de Abstract. We address one of the most fundamental problems concern ing the RSA cryptosystem: does the knowledge of the RSA public and secret key-pair (e, d) yield the factorization of N = pq in polynomial time? It is well-known that there is a probabilistic polynomial time algo rithm that on input (N, e, d) outputs the factors p and q. We present the first deterministic polynomial time algorithm that factors N provided that e, d consists of all integral linear combinations of u1, . . . , uω, that is: L =(X ω i=1 ni· ui| ni ∈ Z) Such a set { u1, . . . , uω} of vectors is called a lattice basis. All the bases have the same number of elements, called the dimension or rank of the lattice. We say that the lattice is full rank if ω = n. Any two bases of the same lattice can be transformed into each other by a multiplication with some integral matrix of determinant ± 1. Therefore, all the bases have the same Gramian determinant det1≤i,j≤d . One defines the determinant of the lattice as the square root of the Gramian determinant. If the lattice is full rank, then the determinant of L is equal to the absolute value of the determinant of the ω × ω matrix whose rows are the basis vectors u1, . . . , uω. The LLL algorithm [4] computes a short vector in a lattice : Theorem 1 (LLL). Let L be a lattice spanned by (u1, . . . , uω). The LLL algo rithm, given (u1, . . . , uω), finds in polynomial time a vector b1 such that: k b1k ≤ 2 (ω−1)/4 det(L) 1/ω 3 The Case of Balanced p and q In this section, we show the deterministic polynomial-time equivalence between recovering d and factoring N, when N is the prod\n", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:49:54.828308+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:1bd75bf14c53ca14f4915299", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "search", "arguments": {"query": "integer factorization worst-case average-case hardness algorithmic classes cryptographic assumptions", "max_results": 5}, "result": "## Search Results (5 results, 309ms)\n\n### 1. Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n- **URL**: https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108\n- # Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach arXiv (Cornell University). Published: 2024-08-28. Preprint. 0 citations. ## Authors - Antoine Joux (Helmholtz Center for Information Security): h-index 41; 7,727 citations - Karol Węgrzycki (Max Planck Institute for Informatics): h-index 7; 126 citations ## Topics - Multi-Criteria Decision Making - Fuzzy Systems and Optimization - Advanced Optimization Algorithms Research ## Funding - Horizon 2020 Framework Programme - European Commission --- # Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm for Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach ## Abstract Lagarias and Odlyzko (J. ACM 1985) proposed a polynomial-time algorithm for solving \"almost all\" instances of the Subset Sum problem with n integers of size Ω(Γ LO ), where log 2 (Γ LO ) > n 2 log 2 (γ) and γ is a parameter of the lattice basis reduction (γ > 4/3 for LLL). The algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is a cornerstone of cryptography. However, the theoretical guarantee on the density of feasible instances has remained unimproved for almost 40 years. In this paper, we propose an algorithm that solves \"almost all\" instances of Subset Sum with integers of size Ω( √ Γ LO ) after a single call to lattice reduction. Additionally, our approach allows solving the Subset Sum problem for multiple targets, whereas the previous method could handle only one target per call to lattice basis reduction. We introduce a modular arithmetic approach to the Subset Sum problem, leveraging lattice reduction to solve a linear system modulo a suitably large prime. By analyzing the lengths of the LLL-reduced basis vectors of both the primal and dual lattices simultaneously, we show that density guarantees can be improved. ## Introduction The Subset Sum problem, a fundamental problem of computational complexity and cryptography, has been extensively studied due to its numerous practical implications. In the Subset Sum problem, we are given n positive integers a 1 , . . . , a n ∈ Z and a target T ∈ Z. The task is to decide if there exists a subset of the given numbers that sum to T . In particular, the study of Subset Sum has seen numerous applications in cryptography [41, 23] , balancing [20] , and combinatorial optimization [36] . With these applications in mind, our goal is to propose an efficient algorithm to solve the Subset Sum for a large range of instances. Indeed, since the problem is NP-hard, it is unlikely that we could propose an efficient algorithm to solve all instances of the problem. The natural next step is to characterize which instances of the problem can be solved efficiently. On that front, there is a classical result by Bellman [11] that proposes a polynomial time algorithm for Subset Sum when the numbers are polynomially bounded, i.e., the instance is dense. This result inspired a long line of pseudopolynomial-time algorithms (see e.g., [17, 37, 45] ). At the other end of the spectrum, Lagarias and Odlyzko [38] introduced a polynomial time algorithm for sparse instances. Their work marked a significant milestone by proposing a polynomial-time algorithm capable of solving \"almost all\" instances of Subset Sum when the numbers are of size Ω(Γ LO ), where log 2 (Γ LO ) > n 2 log 2 γ and γ is a parameter associated with the lattice reduction. Lagarias and Odlyzko [38] employed the LLL (Lenstra-Lenstra-Lovász [40] ) algorithm for lattice basis reduction, for which γ = γ LLL > 4/3. To this day, the Lagarias and Odlyzko algorithm remains a cornerstone of modern cryptography and is featured in numerous text-books on the field [26, 15, 43] . This algorithm was subsequently implemented [46, 49, 16] , simplified [24] and was improved assuming access to an exact Closest Vector Problem (CVP) [22, 34] . Nevertheless, the algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is currently the only known method 1 to solve low-density instances of Subset Sum, both in theory and practice (see [44] for surv\n\n### 2. NP-Complete Sets for Computing Discrete Logarithms and Integer Factorization\n- **URL**: https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/iis/19/2/19_IIS190202/_pdf/-char/ja\n- #### NP-Complete Sets for Computing Discrete Logarithms and Integer Factorization Shingo HASEGAWA , Shuji ISOBE, and Hiroki SHIZUYA Graduate School of Information Sciences, Tohoku University, Sendai 980-8579, Japan Received July 14, 2011; final version accepted March 16, 2013 We explore an NP-complete set such that the problem of breaking a cryptographic scheme reduces to the complete set, where the reduction can be given in a straightforward form like the reduction from the graph isomorphism to the subgraph isomorphism. We construct such NP-complete sets DL and IF for the discrete logarithm problem modulo a prime and the integer factoring problem, respectively. We also show that the decision version of Diffie-Hellman problem reduces directly to DL with respect to the polynomial-time many-one reducibility. These are the first complete sets that have direct reductions from significant cryptographic primitives. KEYWORDS: computational complexity, cryptography ##### 1. Introduction Difficulty of solving a problem can be characterized by the terminology of computational complexity theory. To see this in the context of cryptography, define the following set: DL ¼ fð p; g; y; kÞj9 x½ð y g x (mod pÞÞ ^ ð x kÞg ; where p is a prime. This set is associated with a typical cryptographic primitive called the discrete logarithm problem: given ð p; g; yÞ , find x such that y g x (mod pÞ if such an x exists. Quantum computer can easily solve it in theory [17]. However, in a practical sense, we have no efficient algorithm for this problem, and therefore it is often used to construct cryptographic schemes to ensure the difficulty of breaking the systems. Assume now that there is a polynomial-time algorithm M that on input ð p; g; y; kÞ , outputs 1 if ð p; g; y; kÞ 2 DL, or 0 otherwise. Then the discrete logarithms modulo p can also be computed in polynomial time by calling M as a subroutine polynomially many times. Therefore the set DL is thought to express the difficulty of the discrete logarithm problem. This characterization originated in [2], and has been applied to other discrete logarithm problems [13, 14, 19]. Similarly, the following set corresponds to the integer factoring problem. IF ¼ fð n; kÞj9 d½ð 1 < gcdð n; dÞ < nÞ^ð d kÞg ; where n is an integer to factor. It is clearly seen that DL and IF are sets in NP, and their complements are also in NP. Hence both DL and IF are in NP \\ co-NP. If either DL or IF is complete for NP, then the polynomial-time hierarchy collapses to NP, which is believed to be an unlikely event. In this way, the difficulty of computing discrete logarithms modulo a prime and factoring integers have been characterized by some sets and their complexity classes. Now we recall that DL and IF are in NP. Thus DL and IF reduce to some NP-complete set. We then consider the following question: Which NP-complete set is appropriate to relate with DL or IF? The question above could sound nonsense because any set in NP reduces to any NP-complete set, say SAT (i.e., the set of satisfiable Boolean formulas in conjunctive normal form). That is, for a set A in NP and any string x, one can obtain a Boolean formula ’x in time polynomial in the length of x, such that x 2 A if and only if ’x 2 SAT. However, this generic reduction is very abstract and complicated, and hence we cannot capture the relationship between cryptographic primitives and NP-complete sets. Below we describe a relationship between the graph isomorphism (GI) and the subgraph isomorphism (SubGI) as a comprehensive example illustrating a simple reduction. Let G1 and G2 be two graphs. For simplicity, we restrict ourselves to the graphs that are vertex-labeled, connected and simple. Further, without loss of generality, we assume that the set of vertices and the number of edges of G1 are the same as those of G2, namely G1 ¼ ð V; E1Þ and G2 ¼ ð V; E2Þ with #E1 ¼ #E2. Then we say that G1 and G2 are isomorphic, if there exists a permutation on V\n\n### 3. Measures∗\n- **URL**: https://cims.nyu.edu/~regev/papers/average.pdf\n- #### Worst-case to Average-case Reductions based on Gaussian Measures∗ Daniele Micciancio † Oded Regev ‡ December 14, 2005 Abstract We show that finding small solutions to random modular linear equations is at least as hard as approximating several lattice problems in the worst case within a factor almost linear in the dimension of the lattice. The lattice problems we consider are the shortest vector problem, the shortest independent vectors problem, the covering radius problem, and the guaranteed distance decoding problem (a variant of the well known closest vector problem). The approximation factor we obtain is n log O(1) n for all four problems. This greatly improves on all previous work on the subject starting from Ajtai’s seminal paper (STOC, 1996), up to the strongest previously known results by Micciancio (SIAM J. on Computing, 2004). Our results also bring us closer to the limit where the problems are no longer known to be in NP intersect coNP. Our main tools are Gaussian measures on lattices and the high-dimensional Fourier transform. We start by defining a new lattice parameter which determines the amount of Gaussian noise that one has to add to a lattice in order to get close to a uniform distribution. In addition to yielding quantitatively much stronger results, the use of this parameter allows us to simplify many of the complications in previous work. Our technical contributions are two-fold. First, we show tight connections between this new parameter and existing lattice parameters. One such important connection is between this parameter and the length of the shortest set of linearly independent vectors. Second, we prove that the distribution that one obtains after adding Gaussian noise to the lattice has the following interesting property: the distribution of the noise vector when conditioning on the final value behaves in many respects like the original Gaussian noise vector. In particular, its moments remain essentially unchanged. ##### 1 Introduction Lattice problems have received considerable attention as a potential source of computational hardness to be used in cryptography, after a breakthrough result of Ajtai [2] showing that if certain lattice problems are computationally hard to solve in the worst case, then average-case one-way functions (a fundamental cryptographic primitive) exist. Ajtai’s one-way function is essentially the generalized subset sum function over the additive group of n-dimensional vectors modulo q: functions are described by m group elements a1, . . . , am ∈ Z n q P , and the associated function maps bit-string x1, . . . , xm ∈ { 0, 1} to fA(x1, . . . , xm) = iaixi. The main worst-case lattice problem considered by Ajtai is that of finding a set of n linearly independent lattice vectors in an arbitrary lattice of length within a polynomial (in n) factor from the ∗A preliminary version of this paper appears in the Proceedings of the 45th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science - FOCS 2004. Rome, Italy. Oct. 2004. IEEE, pp. 372-381. †UC San Diego, La Jolla, CA 92093. E-Mail: daniele@cs.ucsd.edu. Research supported in part by NSF Career Award CCR-0093029 and a Sloan Research Fellowship. Any opinions, findings, and conclusions or recommendations expressed in this material are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily reflect the views of the National Science Foundation. ‡Department of Computer Science, Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv 69978, Israel. Work supported by the Israel Science Foundation, by the Binational Science Foundation, by an Alon Fellowship, by the Army Research Office grant DAAD19-03-1-0082, and by NSF grant CCR-9987845. shortest such set. 1 This problem in turn, is related, using standard techniques, to various other lattice problems, like approximating the length of the shortest nonzero lattice vector in the worst case, within factors polynomial in n. No polynomial time algorithm is known to solve any of these worst-case problems, so it is reas\n\n### 4. Public-key cryptography from different assumptions | Proceedings of the forty-second ACM symposium on Theory of computing\n- **URL**: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1806689.1806715\n- Public-key cryptography from different assumptions | Proceedings of the forty-second ACM symposium on Theory of computing skip to main content Contents ## Abstract This paper attempts to broaden the foundations of public-key cryptography. We construct new public-key encryption schemes based on new hardness-on-average assumptions for natural combinatorial NP-hard optimization problems. We consider the following assumptions: It is infeasible to solve a random set of sparse linear equations mod 2, of which a small fraction is noisy. It is infeasible to distinguish between a random unbalanced bipartite graph, and such a graph in which we \"plant\" at random in the large side a set S with only |S|/3 neighbors. There is a pseudorandom generator in NCz where every output depends on a random constant-size subset of the inputs. We obtain semantically secure public key encryption schemes based on several combinations of these assumptions with different parameters. In particular we obtain public key encryption from Assumption~1 on its own, yielding the first noisy-equations type public key scheme in which the noise rate is higher than one over the square root of the number of equations. We also obtain public-key encryption based on a combination of Assumptions~2 and~3. These are arguably of more \"combinatorial\"/\"private-key\" nature than any assumptions used before for public-key cryptography. Our proof involves novel \"search to decision\" and \"search to prediction\" reductions for sparse noisy linear equations. The strength of our assumptions raise new algorithmic and pseudorandomness questions (and new parameters for old ones). We give some evidence for these assumptions by studying their resistance to certain classes of natural algorithms, including semi-definite programs, ACO circuits, low-degree polynomials, and cycle counting. We also relate our assumptions to other problems such as planted clique and learning juntas. ## Formats available You can view the full content in the following formats: ## References [1] D. Achlioptas and A. Coja-Oghlan. Algorithmic barriers from phase transitions. In FOCS, pages 793--802, 2008. [2] D. Aharonov and O. Regev. Lattice problems in NP intersect coNP. J. ACM, 52:749--765, 2005. [3] M. Ajtai and C. Dwork. A public-key cryptosystem with worst-case/average-case equivalence. In STOC, pages 284--293, 1997. [4] M. Alekhnovich. More on average case vs approximation complexity. In FOCS, pages 298--307, 2003. [5] B. Applebaum, Y. Ishai, and E. Kushilevitz. Cryptography in NC0. SIAM J. Comput, 36(4):845--888, 2006. [6] S. Arora, L. Babai, J. Stern, and Z. Sweedyk. The hardness of approximate optima in lattices, codes, and systems of linear equations. J. Comput. Syst. Sci., 54(2):317--331, 1997. [7] S. Arora, B. Barak, M. Brunnermeier, and R. Ge. Computational complexity and information asymmetry in financial products. In ICS, 2010. [8] B. Barak and M. Mahmoody-Ghidary. Merkle puzzles are optimal -- an O(n2) attack on key exchange from a random oracle. In Proceedings of CRYPTO '09, 2009. [9] A. Bhaskara, M. Charikar, E. Chlamtac, U. Feige, and A. Vijayaraghavan. Detecting high log-density -- an O(n1/4)-approximation for densest k-subgraph. In STOC, 2010. [10] E. Biham, Y. J. Goren, and Y. Ishai. Basing weak public-key cryptography on strong one-way functions. In TCC, volume 4948, pages 55--72, 2008. [11] A. Blum, M. Furst, M. Kearns, and R. J. Lipton. Cryptographic primitives based on hard learning problems. In CRYPTO, pages 278--291, 1994. [12] A. L. Blum. Relevant examples and relevant features: Thoughts from computational learning theory. AAAI Fall Symposium on Relevance, 1994. [13] A. L. Blum and P. Langley. Selection of relevant features and examples in machine learning. Artificial Intelligence, 97(1-2):245--271, 1997. [14] A. Bogdanov and Y. Qiao. On the security of goldreich's one-way function. In APPROX-RANDOM, pages 392--405, 2009. [15] M. Braverman. Poly-logarithmi\n\n### 5. On the concrete hardness of Learning with Errors\n- **URL**: https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/046.pdf\n- #### On the concrete hardness of Learning with Errors Martin R. Albrecht 1, Rachel Player 1, and Sam Scott 1 Information Security Group, Royal Holloway, University of London Abstract. The Learning with Errors (LWE) problem has become a central building block of modern cryptographic constructions. This work collects and presents hardness results for concrete instances of LWE. In particular, we discuss algorithms proposed in the literature and give the expected resources required to run them. We consider both generic instances of LWE as well as small secret variants. Since for several methods of solving LWE we require a lattice reduction step, we also review lattice reduction algorithms and use a refined model for estimating their running times. We also give concrete estimates for various families of LWE instances, provide a Sage module for computing these estimates and highlight gaps in the knowledge about algorithms for solving the Learning with Errors problem. ##### Warning (August 2019) While the LWE Estimator 1, which accompanies this work, has been continuously updated since its publication, this work itself has not been. We therefore caution the reader against using this work as a reference for the state of the art in assessing the cost of solving LWE or making sense of the LWE estimator. For example, we note: – The cost of lattice reduction is estimated differently in the LWE estimator than as described in Section 3. Since commit 9d37ca0, the cost of BKZ in dimension n with block size k is estimated as tBKZ = 8· n· tk, where tk is the cost of calling an SVP oracle in dimension k. The cost estimates for sieving have been updated: the classical estimate follows [BDGL16] since commit 74ee857 and a new quantum sieving estimate following [LMvdP15,Laa15] has been available since commit 509a759. – This paper assumes that the number of LWE samples m is unlimited, which may not represent the situation in practice. Bindel et al. [BBGS17] implemented an extension that enables users to specify a limit on m. This was incorporated into the LWE estimator at commit eb45a74. – Many new variants [GJS15,KF15,GJMS17,EHK +18] of the BKW algorithm (see Section 5.2) have appeared since the time of writing. The LWE estimator supports Coded-BKW [GJS15] since commit c662db9. – Albrecht [Alb17] gave a new variant of the dual attack (see Section 5.3) for situations where the secret is small and/or sparse. Estimates of this attack were incorporated into the LWE estimator at commit 6295aa5. – Cheon et al. showed [CHHS19] that the MITM algorithm given in Section 5.1 is wrong, which results in its success probability being negligibly small in practice. Cheon et al. [CHHS19] presented an alternative MITM algorithm, and show how to combine it with Albrecht’s [Alb17] dual attack to give a new hybrid dual attack. Neither the new MITM algorithm or the hybrid dual attack are incorporated in the LWE estimator as of commit 3019847. – Section 5.5, on the primal uSVP attack, follows the analysis in [GN08,AFG14] for estimating the success of lattice reduction in solving uSVP. It has been shown [AGVW17] that a more recent estimate of Alkim et al. [ADPS16] better models the behaviour of lattice reduction in this case. This estimate has been used in the LWE estimator since commit e7c9c59. 1 https://bitbucket.org/malb/lwe-estimator/ – Neither the LWE estimator nor this work take “dimensions for free” [Duc18,ADH +19] into account. A more recent version of the material presented here is available in Player’s thesis [Pla18]. This was up-to-date as of November 2017, and fixes many typos that are still present in this paper. ##### 1 Introduction Lattice-based cryptography. Lattice-based cryptography has become popular in recent years for several reasons. One dates back to the work of Ajtai [Ajt96] who linked the average case complex ity of lattice problems to the worst case, showing that a random instance of the shortest vector problem i\n", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:00.900756+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:ee67442ef7ff1b002ca83eb9", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://cs.stackexchange.com/questions/50767/why-is-not-known-whether-integer-factorization-can-be-done-in-polynomial-time-kn"}, "result": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:08.993454+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:0f3c50e478deddf62419710f", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test"}, "result": "## AKS primality test - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nAlgorithm checking for prime numbers\n\nThe**AKS primality test**(also known as the**Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena primality test**and the**cyclotomic AKS test**) is a[deterministic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)[primality-proving](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_test)[algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithm)created and published by[Manindra Agrawal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manindra_Agrawal),[Neeraj Kayal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neeraj_Kayal), and[Nitin Saxena](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitin_Saxena), computer scientists at the[Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Institute_of_Technology_Kanpur), on August 6, 2002, in an article titled \"PRIMES is in P\".[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)The algorithm was the first one which is able to determine in[polynomial time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time), whether a given number is[prime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number)or[composite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composite_number)without relying on[mathematical conjectures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_conjecture)such as the[generalized Riemann hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis). The proof is also notable for not relying on the field of[analysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analysis_(mathematics)).[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-2)In 2006 the authors received both the[Gödel Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del_Prize)and[Fulkerson Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulkerson_Prize)for their work.\n\n## Importance[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nAKS is the first primality-proving algorithm to be simultaneously*general*,*polynomial-time*,*deterministic*, and*unconditionally correct*. Previous algorithms had been developed for centuries and achieved three of these properties at most, but not all four.\n- The AKS algorithm can be used to verify the primality of any**general**number given. Many fast primality tests are known that work only for numbers with certain properties. For example, the[Lucas–Lehmer test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas%E2%80%93Lehmer_primality_test)works only for[Mersenne numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mersenne_number), while[Pépin's test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%A9pin%27s_test)can be applied to[Fermat numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number)only.\n- The maximum running time of the algorithm can be bounded by a**[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time#Polynomial_time)**over the number of digits in the target number.[ECPP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve_primality_proving)and[APR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adleman%E2%80%93Pomerance%E2%80%93Rumely_primality_test)conclusively prove or disprove that a given number is prime, but are not known to have polynomial time bounds for all inputs.\n- The algorithm is guaranteed to distinguish**[deterministically](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)**whether the target number is prime or composite. Randomized tests, such as[Miller–Rabin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test)and[Baillie–PSW](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test), can test any given number for primality in polynomial time, but are known to produce only a probabilistic result.\n- The correctness of AKS is**not conditional**on any subsidiary unproved[hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothesis). In contrast, Miller's version of the[Miller–Rabin test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test#Deterministic_variants)is fully deterministic and runs in polynomial time over all inputs, but its correctness depends on the truth of the yet-unproved[generalized Riemann hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis).\n\nWhile the algorithm is of immense theoretical importance, it is not used in practice, rendering it a[galactic algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_algorithm). For 64-bit inputs, the[Baillie–PSW test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test)is deterministic and runs many orders of magnitude faster. For larger inputs, the performance of the (also unconditionally correct) ECPP and APR tests is*far*superior to AKS. Additionally, ECPP can output a[primality certificate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_certificate)that allows independent and rapid verification of the results, which is not possible with the AKS algorithm.\n\n## Concepts[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=2)]\n\nThe AKS primality test is based upon the following theorem: Given an integern≥2{\\displaystyle n\\geq 2}and integera{\\displaystyle a}[coprime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime)ton{\\displaystyle n},n{\\displaystyle n}is prime if and only if the[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)[congruence relation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congruence_relation#Modular_arithmetic)(X+a)n≡Xn+a(modn){\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}\\equiv X^{n}+a{\\pmod {n}}}1\n\nholds within the polynomial ring(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)Note thatX{\\displaystyle X}denotes the[indeterminate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring#Definition_(univariate_case))which generates this polynomial ring.\n\nThis theorem is a generalization to polynomials of[Fermat's little theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat's_little_theorem). In one direction it can easily be proved using the[binomial theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem)together with the following property of the[binomial coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient):(nk)≡0(modn){\\displaystyle {n \\choose k}\\equiv 0{\\pmod {n}}}for all0<k<n{\\displaystyle 0<k<n}ifn{\\displaystyle n}is prime.\n\nWhile the relation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_1)**) constitutes a primality test in itself, verifying it takes[exponential time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_time): the[brute force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute_force_method)approach would require the expansion of the(X+a)n{\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}}polynomial and a reduction(modn){\\displaystyle {\\pmod {n}}}of the resultingn+1{\\displaystyle n+1}coefficients.\n\nThe congruence is an equality in the[polynomial ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}. Evaluating in a quotient ring of(Z/nZ)[X]{\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}creates an upper bound for the degree of the polynomials involved. The AKS evaluates the equality in(Z/nZ)[X]/(Xr−1){\\displaystyle (\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]/(X^{r}-1)}, making the[computational complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_complexity_theory)dependent on the size ofr{\\displaystyle r}. For clarity,[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)this is expressed as the congruence(X+a)n≡(Xn+a)(modXr−1,n){\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}\\equiv (X^{n}+a){\\pmod {X^{r}-1,n}}}2\n\nwhich is the same as:(X+a)n−(Xn+a)=(Xr−1)g+nf{\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}-(X^{n}+a)=(X^{r}-1)g+nf}3\n\nfor some polynomialsf{\\displaystyle f}andg{\\displaystyle g}.\n\nNote that all primes satisfy this relation (choosingg=0{\\displaystyle g=0}in (**[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_3)**) gives (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_1)**), which holds forn{\\displaystyle n}prime). This congruence can be checked in polynomial time whenr{\\displaystyle r}is polynomial to the digits ofn{\\displaystyle n}. The AKS algorithm evaluates this congruence for a large set ofa{\\displaystyle a}values, whose size is polynomial to the digits ofn{\\displaystyle n}. The proof of validity of the AKS algorithm shows that one can find anr{\\displaystyle r}and a set ofa{\\displaystyle a}values with the above properties such that if the congruences hold thenn{\\displaystyle n}is a power of a prime.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)\n\n## History and running time[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=3)]\n\nIn the first version of the above-cited paper, the authors proved the asymptotic time complexity of the algorithm to beO~(log⁡(n)12){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{12})}(using[Õ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_O_notation#Extensions_to_the_Bachmann%E2%80%93Landau_notations)from[big O notation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_O_notation))—the twelfth power of the number of digits in*n*times a factor that is polylogarithmic in the number of digits. However, this upper bound was rather loose; a widely-held conjecture about the distribution of the[Sophie Germain primes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_Germain_prime)would, if true, immediately cut the worst case down toO~(log⁡(n)6){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{6})}.\n\nIn the months following the discovery, new variants appeared (Lenstra 2002, Pomerance 2002, Berrizbeitia 2002, Cheng 2003, Bernstein 2003a/b, Lenstra and Pomerance 2003), which improved the speed of computation greatly. Owing to the existence of the many variants, Crandall and Papadopoulos refer to the \"AKS-class\" of algorithms in their scientific paper \"On the implementation of AKS-class primality tests\", published in March 2003.\n\nIn response to some of these variants, and to other feedback, the paper \"PRIMES is in P\" was updated with a new formulation of the AKS algorithm and of its proof of correctness. (This version was eventually published in*[Annals of Mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annals_of_Mathematics)*.) While the basic idea remained the same,*r*was chosen in a new manner, and the proof of correctness was more coherently organized. The new proof relied almost exclusively on the behavior of[cyclotomic polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclotomic_polynomial)over[finite fields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_fields). The new upper bound on time complexity wasO~(log⁡(n)10.5){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{10.5})}, later reduced using additional results from[sieve theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieve_theory)toO~(log⁡(n)7.5){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{7.5})}.\n\nIn 2005,[Pomerance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Pomerance)and[Lenstra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hendrik_Lenstra)demonstrated a variant of AKS that runs inO~(log⁡(n)6){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{6})}operations,[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-lenstra_pomerance_2005-3)leading to another updated version of the paper.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-lenstra_pomerance_2011-4)Agrawal, Kayal and Saxena proposed a variant which would run inO~(log⁡(n)3){\\displaystyle {\\tilde {O}}(\\log(n)^{3})}if[Agrawal's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agrawal's_conjecture)were true; however, a heuristic argument by Pomerance and Lenstra suggested that it is probably false.\n\n## The algorithm[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=4)]\n\nThe algorithm is as follows:[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)Input: integer*n*>1.\n- Check if*n*is a[perfect power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfect_power): if*n*=*a**b*for integers*a*>1and*b*>1, then output*composite*.\n- Find the smallest*r*such that[ord](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_order)*r*(*n*)>(log2*n*)2. If*r*and*n*are not coprime, then output*composite*.\n- For all 2 ≤*a*≤ min (*r*,*n*−1), check that*a*does not divide*n*: If*a*|*n*for some 2 ≤*a*≤ min (*r*,*n*−1), then output*composite*.\n- If*n*≤*r*, then output*prime*.\n- For*a*=1to⌊φ(r)log2⁡(n)⌋{\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor {\\sqrt {\\varphi (r)}}\\log _{2}(n)\\right\\rfloor }doif (*X*+*a*)*n*≠*X**n*+*a*(mod*X**r*− 1,*n*), then output*composite*;\n- Output*prime*.\n\nHere[ord](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_order)*r*(*n*) is the[multiplicative order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_order)of*n*[modulo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_arithmetic)*r*, log2is the[binary logarithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_logarithm), andφ(r){\\displaystyle \\varphi (r)}is[Euler's totient function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler's_totient_function)of*r*.\n\nStep 3 is shown in the paper as checking 1 < gcd(*a*,*n*) <*n*for all*a*≤*r*. It can be seen this is equivalent to trial division up to*r*, which can be done very efficiently without using[gcd](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor). Similarly the comparison in step 4 can be replaced by having the trial division return*prime*once it has checked all values up to and including⌊n⌋.{\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor {\\sqrt {n}}\\right\\rfloor .}\n\nOnce beyond very small inputs, step 5 dominates the time taken. The essential reduction in complexity (from exponential to polynomial) is achieved by performing all calculations in the finite ringR=(Z/nZ)[X]/(Xr−1){\\displaystyle R=(\\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} )[X]/(X^{r}-1)}\n\nconsisting ofnr{\\displaystyle n^{r}}elements. This ring contains only ther{\\displaystyle r}[monomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monomial){X0,X1,…,Xr−1}{\\displaystyle \\{X^{0},X^{1},\\ldots ,X^{r-1}\\}}, and the[coefficients](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coefficient)are inZ/nZ{\\displaystyle \\mathbb {Z} /n\\mathbb {Z} }which hasn{\\displaystyle n}elements, all of them codable withinlog2⁡(n){\\displaystyle \\log _{2}(n)}bits.\n\nMost later improvements made to the algorithm have concentrated on reducing the size of*r,*which makes the core operation in step 5 faster, and in reducing the size of*s*, the number of loops performed in step 5.[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-bernstein03-5)Typically these changes do not change the computational complexity, but can lead to many orders of magnitude less time taken; for example, Bernstein's final version has a theoretical speedup by a factor of over 2 million.\n\n### Proof of validity outline[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=5)]\n\nFor the algorithm to be correct, all steps that identify*n*must be correct. Steps 1, 3, and 4 are trivially correct, since they are based on direct tests of the divisibility of*n*. Step 5 is also correct: since (2) is true for any choice of*a*coprime to*n*and*r*if*n*is prime, an inequality means that*n*must be composite.\n\nThe difficult part of the proof is showing that step 6 is true. Its proof of correctness is based on the upper and lower bounds of a[multiplicative group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_group)inZn[x]{\\displaystyle \\mathbb {Z} _{n}[x]}constructed from the (*X*+*a*) binomials that are tested in step 5. Step 4 guarantees that these binomials are⌊φ(r)log2⁡(n)⌋{\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor {\\sqrt {\\varphi (r)}}\\log _{2}(n)\\right\\rfloor }distinct elements ofZn[x]{\\displaystyle \\mathbb {Z} _{n}[x]}. For the particular choice of*r*, the bounds produce a[contradiction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_by_contradiction)unless*n*is prime or a power of a prime. Together with the test of step 1, this implies that*n*is always prime at step 6.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)\n\n### Example 1:*n*= 31 is prime[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=6)]**Input**: integer*n*= 31 > 1.(* Step 1 *)**If**(*n*=*a**b*for integers*a*> 1 and*b*> 1),**output***composite*.**For**( b = 2; b <= log2(n); b++) { a = n1/b;**If**(a is integer),**Return**[Composite] } a = n1/2...n1/4= {5.568, 3.141, 2.360}(* Step 2 *)Find the smallest*r*such that*O**r*(*n*) > (log*2**n*)2. maxk = ⌊(log*2*n)2⌋; maxr = Max[3, ⌈(Log*2*n)5⌉];(* maxr really isn't needed *)nextR = True;**For**(r = 2; nextR && r < maxr; r++) { nextR = False;**For**(k = 1; (!nextR) && k≤maxk; k++) { nextR = (Mod[nk, r] == 1 || Mod[nk, r]==0) } } r--;(*the loop over increments by one*)r = 29(* Step 3 *)**If**(1 <[gcd](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor)(*a*,*n*) <*n*for some*a*≤*r*),**output***composite*.**For**(a = r; a>1; a--) {**If**((gcd = GCD[a,n])>1 && gcd < n),**Return**[Composite] } gcd = {GCD(29,31)=1, GCD(28,31)=1, ..., GCD(2,31)=1} ≯ 1(* Step 4 *)**If**(*n*≤*r*),**output***prime*.**If**(n≤r),**Return**[Prime](* this step may be omitted if n>5690034 *)31>29(* Step 5 *)**For***a*= 1**to**⌊φ(r)log2⁡(n)⌋{\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor {\\sqrt {\\varphi (r)}}\\log _{2}(n)\\right\\rfloor }**do****If**((*X*+*a*)*n*≠*X**n*+*a*(mod*X**r*− 1,*n*)),**output***composite*; φ[x_] := EulerPhi[x]; PolyModulo[f_] := PolynomialMod[[PolynomialRemainder](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_remainder)[f, xr-1, x], n]; max = Floor[Log[2, n]√φ[r]];**For**(a = 1; a ≤ max; a++) {**If**(PolyModulo[(x+a)n- PolynomialRemainder[xn+a, xr-1, x]] ≠ 0) {**Return**[Composite] { } (x+a)31= a31+31a30x  [(x+a)31, x29-1] = 465a2+a31+(31a+31a30)x +(1+465a29)(A) PolynomialMod [PolynomialRemainder [(x+a)31, x29-1], 31] = a31+x2(B) PolynomialRemainder [x31+a, x29-1] = a+x2(**[A](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_A)**) - (**[B](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_B)**) = a31+x2- (a+x2) = a31-amax=⌊log2⁡(31)φ(29)⌋=26{\\displaystyle \\max =\\left\\lfloor \\log _{2}(31){\\sqrt {\\varphi (29)}}\\right\\rfloor =26}{131-1 = 0 (mod 31), 231-2 = 0 (mod 31), 331-3 = 0 (mod 31), ..., 2631-26 = 0 (mod 31)}(* Step 6 *)**Output***prime*.31 Must be Prime\n\nWhere PolynomialMod is a term-wise modulo reduction of the polynomial. e.g. PolynomialMod[x+2x2+3x3, 3] = x+2x2+0x3\n\n[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-6)\n\n## References[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=7)]\n\n- [1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-1)[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-2)[4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-3)[5](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-4)[6](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-AKS_1-5)Agrawal, Manindra; Kayal, Neeraj; Saxena, Nitin (2004).[\"PRIMES is in P\"](http://www.cse.iitk.ac.in/users/manindra/algebra/primality_v6.pdf)(PDF).*[Annals of Mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annals_of_Mathematics)*.**160**(2):781–793.[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.4007/annals.2004.160.781](https://doi.org/10.4007%2Fannals.2004.160.781).[JSTOR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSTOR_(identifier))[3597229](https://www.jstor.org/stable/3597229).\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-2)Granville, Andrew (2005).[\"It is easy to determine whether a given integer is prime\"](https://www.ams.org/bull/2005-42-01/S0273-0979-04-01037-7/home.html).*Bull. Amer. Math. Soc*.**42**:3–38.[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.1090/S0273-0979-04-01037-7](https://doi.org/10.1090%2FS0273-0979-04-01037-7).\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-lenstra_pomerance_2005_3-0)H. W. Lenstra Jr. and Carl Pomerance, \"[Primality testing with Gaussian periods](http://www.math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/complexity12.pdf)\", preliminary version July 20, 2005.\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-lenstra_pomerance_2011_4-0)H. W. Lenstra Jr. and Carl Pomerance, \"[Primality testing with Gaussian periods](http://www.math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/aks041411.pdf)[Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20120225052810/http://www.math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/aks041411.pdf)2012-02-25 at the[Wayback Machine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine)\", version of April 12, 2011.\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-bernstein03_5-0)Daniel J. Bernstein, \"[Proving Primality After Agrawal-Kayal-Saxena](https://cr.yp.to/papers/aks.pdf)\", version of January 25, 2003.\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_ref-6)See[AKS Talk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:AKS_primality_test#Worked_Example)page for a discussion on why 'Example 2: n is not Prime past Step 4' is missing.\n\n## Further reading[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=8)]\n- Dietzfelbinger, Martin (2004).*Primality testing in polynomial time. From randomized algorithms to*PRIMES is in P**. Lecture Notes in Computer Science. Vol.3000. Berlin:[Springer-Verlag](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springer-Verlag).[ISBN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier))[3-540-40344-2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/3-540-40344-2).[Zbl](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zbl_(identifier))[1058.11070](https://zbmath.org/?format=complete&q=an:1058.11070).\n\n## External links[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=9)]\n- [Weisstein, Eric W.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_W._Weisstein)[\"AKS Primality Test\"](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/AKSPrimalityTest.html).*[MathWorld](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MathWorld)*.\n- [R. Crandall, Apple ACG, and J. Papadopoulos (March 18, 2003): On the implementation of AKS-class primality tests](https://web.archive.org/web/20140219064936/http://www.dm.unito.it/~cerruti/ac/aks-crandall.pdf)(PDF)\n- [Article by Bornemann, containing photos and information about the three Indian scientists](https://www.ams.org/notices/200305/fea-bornemann.pdf)(PDF)\n- [Andrew Granville: It is easy to determine whether a given integer is prime](https://www.ams.org/bull/2005-42-01/S0273-0979-04-01037-7/home.html)\n- [The Prime Facts: From Euclid to AKS](http://www.scottaaronson.com/writings/prime.pdf), by[Scott Aaronson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scott_Aaronson)(PDF)\n- [The PRIMES is in P little FAQ](http://www.instantlogic.net/publications/PRIMES%20is%20in%20P%20little%20FAQ.htm)by Anton Stiglic\n- [2006 Gödel Prize Citation](https://web.archive.org/web/20150327071905/http://www.sigact.org/Prizes/Godel/2006.html)\n- [2006 Fulkerson Prize Citation](https://www.ams.org/notices/200611/comm-fulkerson.pdf)\n- [The AKS \"PRIMES in P\" Algorithm Resource](http://fatphil.org/maths/AKS)\n\n- [v](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Number-theoretic_algorithms)\n- [t](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Number-theoretic_algorithms)\n- [e](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Number-theoretic_algorithms)\n\n[Number-theoretic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory)[algorithms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithm)[Primality tests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_test)\n\n- [AKS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)\n- [APR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adleman%E2%80%93Pomerance%E2%80%93Rumely_primality_test)\n- [Baillie–PSW](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test)\n- [Elliptic curve](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve_primality)\n- [Pocklington](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocklington_primality_test)\n- [Fermat](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_primality_test)\n- [Lucas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas_primality_test)\n- *[Lucas–Lehmer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas%E2%80%93Lehmer_primality_test)*\n- *[Lucas–Lehmer–Riesel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas%E2%80%93Lehmer%E2%80%93Riesel_test)*\n- *[Proth's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proth's_theorem)*\n- *[Pépin's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%A9pin%27s_test)*\n- [Quadratic Frobenius](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_Frobenius_test)\n- [Solovay–Strassen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solovay%E2%80%93Strassen_primality_test)\n- [Miller–Rabin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test)[Prime-generating](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generating_primes)\n\n- [Sieve of Atkin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieve_of_Atkin)\n- [Sieve of Eratosthenes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieve_of_Eratosthenes)\n- [Sieve of Pritchard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieve_of_Pritchard)\n- [Sieve of Sundaram](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sieve_of_Sundaram)\n- [Wheel factorization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel_factorization)[Integer factorization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_factorization)\n\n- [Continued fraction (CFRAC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continued_fraction_factorization)\n- [Dixon's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixon's_factorization_method)\n- [Lenstra elliptic curve (ECM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenstra_elliptic-curve_factorization)\n- [Euler's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler's_factorization_method)\n- [Pollard's rho](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollard's_rho_algorithm)\n- [*p*− 1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollard%27s_p_%E2%88%92_1_algorithm)\n- [*p*+ 1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williams's_p_+_1_algorithm)\n- [Quadratic sieve (QS)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_sieve)\n- [General number field sieve (GNFS)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_number_field_sieve)\n- *[Special number field sieve (SNFS)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_number_field_sieve)*\n- [Rational sieve](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rational_sieve)\n- [Fermat's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat's_factorization_method)\n- [Shanks's square forms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanks's_square_forms_factorization)\n- [Trial division](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trial_division)\n- [Shor's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shor's_algorithm)[Multiplication](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplication_algorithm)\n\n- [Ancient Egyptian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_multiplication)\n- [Long](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_multiplication)\n- [Karatsuba](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karatsuba_algorithm)\n- [Toom–Cook](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toom%E2%80%93Cook_multiplication)\n- [Schönhage–Strassen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sch%C3%B6nhage%E2%80%93Strassen_algorithm)\n- [Fürer's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%BCrer%27s_algorithm)[Euclidean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_division)[division](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Division_algorithm)\n\n- [Binary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_division)\n- [Chunking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chunking_(division))\n- [Fourier](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourier_division)\n- [Goldschmidt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldschmidt_division)\n- [Newton-Raphson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton%E2%80%93Raphson_division)\n- [Long](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_division)\n- [Short](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short_division)\n- [SRT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SRT_division)[Discrete logarithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_logarithm)\n\n- [Baby-step giant-step](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby-step_giant-step)\n- [Pollard rho](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollard's_rho_algorithm_for_logarithms)\n- [Pollard kangaroo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollard's_kangaroo_algorithm)\n- [Pohlig–Hellman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pohlig%E2%80%93Hellman_algorithm)\n- [Index calculus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Index_calculus_algorithm)\n- [Function field sieve](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_field_sieve)[Greatest common divisor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor)\n\n- [Binary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_GCD_algorithm)\n- [Euclidean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm)\n- [Extended Euclidean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extended_Euclidean_algorithm)\n- [Lehmer's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lehmer's_GCD_algorithm)[Modular square root](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_residue)\n\n- [Cipolla](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cipolla's_algorithm)\n- [Pocklington's](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocklington's_algorithm)\n- [Tonelli–Shanks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonelli%E2%80%93Shanks_algorithm)\n- [Berlekamp](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlekamp%E2%80%93Rabin_algorithm)Other algorithms\n\n- [Chakravala](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chakravala_method)\n- [Cornacchia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornacchia's_algorithm)\n- [Exponentiation by squaring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponentiation_by_squaring)\n- [Integer square root](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_square_root)\n- [Integer relation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_relation_algorithm)([LLL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenstra%E2%80%93Lenstra%E2%80%93Lov%C3%A1sz_lattice_basis_reduction_algorithm);[KZ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korkine%E2%80%93Zolotarev_lattice_basis_reduction_algorithm))\n- [Modular exponentiation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_exponentiation)\n- [Montgomery reduction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgomery_reduction)\n- [Schoof](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schoof's_algorithm)\n- [Trachtenberg system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trachtenberg_system)\n\n- *Italics*indicate that algorithm is for numbers of special forms\n\nRetrieved from \"[https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&oldid=1345615643](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&oldid=1345615643)\"\n\n[Categories](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Category):\n- [Indian inventions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Indian_inventions)\n- [Primality tests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Primality_tests)\n- [Finite fields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Finite_fields)\n\nHidden categories:\n- [Articles with short description](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Articles_with_short_description)\n- [Short description is different from Wikidata](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Short_description_is_different_from_Wikidata)\n- [Webarchive template wayback links](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Webarchive_template_wayback_links)\n- [Articles with example pseudocode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Articles_with_example_pseudocode)\n\nAKS primality test\n\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)\n\n[Add topic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test)", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:14.892597+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:900ef55a69e49e9f8bb7b5df", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334"}, "result": "## Client Challenge\n\n**Source**: https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334\n\n---\n\nA required part of this site couldn’t load. This may be due to a browser extension, network issues, or browser settings. Please check your connection, disable any ad blockers, or try using a different browser.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:20.509736+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:cc35f8296115abd79497261a", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://academicweb.nd.edu/~andyp/notes/AKS.pdf"}, "result": "extract_unsupported_content\nResponse content type is not supported.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:26.331465+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:f27a22657e5df0ada58ef505", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2004/208.pdf"}, "result": "extract_unsupported_content\nResponse content type is not supported.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:50:31.495820+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:2bb314113beae3a139b56120", "attempt_id": "attempt:23e1183b540167a2b55dd9ff", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108"}, "result": "## Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\n**Source**: https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108\n\n---\n\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit![Learn more](https://info.arxiv.org/about)\n\n[License: CC Zero](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2408.16108v1 [cs.DS] 28 Aug 2024\n\n# Improving Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm For Average-Case Subset Sum: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\nAntoine JouxNote:CISPA Helmholtz Center for Information Security, Germany,joux@cipa.de. This work has been supported by the European Union’s H2020 Programme under grant agreement number ERC-669891.Karol WęgrzyckiNote:Saarland University and Max Planck Institute for Informatics, Saarbrücken, Germany,wegrzycki@cs.uni-saarland.de. This work is part of the project TIPEA that has received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement No 850979).\n\n###### Abstract\n\nLagarias and Odlyzko (J. ACM 1985) proposed a polynomial time algorithm for solving “*almost all*” instances of the Subset Sum problem withnnintegers of sizeΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}), wherelog2⁡(ΓLO)>n2​log2⁡(γ)\\log_{2}(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})>n^{2}\\log_{2}(\\gamma)andγ\\gammais a parameter of the lattice basis reduction (γ>4/3\\gamma>\\sqrt{4/3}for LLL). The algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is a cornerstone result in cryptography. However, the theoretical guarantee on the density of feasible instances has remained unimproved for almost 40 years.\n\nIn this paper, we propose an algorithm to solve “*almost all*” instances of Subset Sum with integers of sizeΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}})after a single call to the lattice reduction. Additionally, our argument allows us to solve the Subset Sum problem for multiple targets while the previous approach could only answer one target per call to lattice basis reduction. We introduce a modular arithmetic approach to the Subset Sum problem. The idea is to use the lattice reduction to solve a linear system modulo a suitably large prime. We show that density guarantees can be improved, by analysing the lengths of the LLL reduced basis vectors, of both the primal and the dual lattices simultaneously.\n\n## 1Introduction\n\nThe Subset Sum problem, a cornerstone of computational complexity and cryptography, has been the subject of extensive research due to numerous practical implications. In the Subset Sum problem, we are givennnpositive integersa1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}and a targetT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}. The task is to decide if there exists a subset of the given numbers that sums toTT.\n\nIn particular, the study of Subset Sum has seen numerous applications in cryptography[[36](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib36),[18](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib18)], balancing[[15](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib15)], and combinatorial optimization[[29](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib29)]. With these applications in mind, our goal is to propose an efficient algorithm to solve the Subset Sum for a large range of instances. Indeed, since the problem is NP-hard, it is very unlikely that we could propose an efficient algorithm to solve all instances of the problem. The natural next step is to characterize which instances of the problem can be solved efficiently. On that front, there is a classical result of Bellman[[9](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib9)]that proposes a polynomial time algorithm for Subset Sum when the numbers are polynomially bounded, i.e., the instance is*dense*. This inspired a long line of pseudopolynomial-time algorithms (see e.g.,[[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib12),[30](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib30),[1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib1),[40](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib40),[34](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib34)]).\n\nOn the other end of the spectrum, there is a result by Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]for the*sparse*instances. This work marked a significant milestone by proposing a polynomial-time algorithm capable of solving “*almost all*” instances of Subset Sum when the size of the numbers isΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}), wherelog2⁡(ΓLO)>n2​log2​γ\\log_{2}(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})>n^{2}\\log_{2}{\\gamma}andγ\\gammais a parameter associated with the lattice reduction. Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]used the LLL (Lenstra-Lenstra-Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]) algorithm as a lattice basis reduction, for whichγ=γLLL>4/3\\gamma=\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}.\n\nTo this day, the algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is a cornerstone of modern cryptography and is featured in numerous text-books on the field[[21](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib21),[10](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib10),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)]. This algorithm was subsequently implemented[[41](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib41),[44](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib44),[11](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib11)], simplified[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]and even improved assuming access to an exact CVP[[17](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib17),[28](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib28)]. Nevertheless, the algorithm of Lagarias and Odlyzko is currently the only known method111There are several extensions of the LLL algorithm tailored for solving Subset Sum in practice, see[[44](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib44),[32](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib32)].to solve low-density instances of Subset Sum, both in theory and practice (see[[39](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib39)]for survey).\n\nIn this paper, we revisit and improve the result of Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]. We show an algorithm solving almost all instances of Subset Sum when the size of the numbers isΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}). More formally, we prove the following theorem.\n\n###### Theorem 1.1.\n\nLeta1,…,an∈ℤ⩾0a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{\\geqslant 0}be integers, selected uniformly at random from{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}, whereΓLO=(γLLL)n2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}=(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}. With overwhelming probability, after a single call to a basis reduction algorithm with parameterγLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}, we obtain a polynomial time tester, that for every givenT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}decides whetherthere exist​e1,…,en∈{0,1}​such that​∑i=1nei⋅ai=T.\\text{there exist }e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ such that }\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}\\cdot a_{i}=T.\n\nOur tester and its creation method are both deterministic. Their success probability only depends on the random choice of the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}and is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nThe improvement offered by[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)is two-fold. First, in terms of admissible range, our algorithm provides improvement fromΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}})down toΩ⁡(ΓLO)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}})on numbers in the instance. Second, our algorithm is*oblivious*to the target. Namely, after a single call to the lattice basis reduction, with input numbersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}we can deterministically decide on an answer in polynomial time. In that sense, our argument provides a succinct certificate for “almost all” instances of sparse Subset Sum. Precisely this question was already considered by Furst and Kannan[[20](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib20)]who used lattice basis reduction to bound the proof complexity of Subset Sum. They strengthened the Lagarias and Odlyzko algorithm and showed that “almost all” instances with numbers in{1,…,ΓLO}\\{1,\\ldots,\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}\\}have a polynomial size proof. Our argument again reduces the range down to{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}, and our certificates are simpler. In fact, the data-structure behind[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)invokes a single matrix-vector multiplication.\n\n### 1.1Our Technique: Modular Arithmetic Approach\n\nNow, we elaborate on the techniques that we introduce. In this note, we let⟦a⟧p\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{p}be a unique integer in(−p/2,p/2)(-p/2,p/2)such thata≡⟦a⟧p​(mod​p)a\\equiv\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{p}\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ p)(see[Section2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2)for notation). Consider vectorsa=(a1,…,an)∈ℤn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}=(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n},e=(e1,…,en)∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}and an integerTTsuch thate1⋅a1+e2⋅a2+…+en⋅an\\displaystyle e_{1}\\cdot a_{1}+e_{2}\\cdot a_{2}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot a_{n}=T.\\displaystyle=T.Obviously, for any integersμ,p∈ℤ\\mu,p\\in\\mathbb{Z}the following also holdse1⋅⟦μ​a1⟧p+e2⋅⟦μ​a2⟧p+…+en⋅⟦μ​an⟧p\\displaystyle e_{1}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}+e_{2}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}≡⟦μ​T⟧p​(mod​p).\\displaystyle\\equiv\\llbracket\\mu T\\rrbracket_{p}\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ p).\n\nNext, assume, that we have managed to findμ\\muandpp, such that:∑i=1n|⟦μ​ai⟧p|<p/2.\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\\llbracket\\mu a_{i}\\rrbracket_{p}|<p/2.\n\nThen there are no overflows moduloppand actually, we can writee1⋅⟦μ​a1⟧p+e2⋅⟦μ​a2⟧p+…+en⋅⟦μ​an⟧p=⟦μ​T⟧p.e_{1}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}+e_{2}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}+\\ldots+e_{n}\\cdot\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}=\\llbracket\\mu T\\rrbracket_{p}.\n\nWe will show that if the density is small, then there are integersμ1,…,μn∈ℤ\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that for each of them*(a)*theℓ1\\ell_{1}norm is bounded, i.e.,‖⟦μi​a⟧p‖1<p/2\\left\\lVert\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}\\right\\rVert_{1}<p/2, and*(b)*the vectors⟦μi​a⟧p\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}are linearly independent. More formally we will prove the following theorem.\n\n###### Theorem 1.2(Modular Arithmetic Approach).\n\nLetΓLO≔Θ⁡((γLLL)n2⋅24​n​log2⁡n)\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}\\coloneqq\\Theta((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{4n\\log_{2}{n}})and letp=Θ⁡((γLLL)0.5​n2)p=\\Theta((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{0.5n^{2}})be a prime. There exists a polynomial time algorithm, that given integersa1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}selected uniformly at random from{1,…,⌈ΓLO⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\right\\rceil\\}outputs, with overwhelming probability, integersμ1,…,μn∈ℤ\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that the followingn×nn\\times nmatrixℳp≔(⟦μ1​a1⟧p⟦μ1​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ1​an⟧p⟦μ2​a1⟧p⟦μ2​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ2​an⟧p⟦μn​a1⟧p⟦μn​a2⟧p⋯⟦μn​an⟧p)\\mathcal{M}_{p}\\coloneqq\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots&&&\\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\n\nhas full rank. Moreover, for every1⩽i⩽n1\\leqslant i\\leqslant nit holds that:‖(⟦μi​a1⟧p,…,⟦μi​an⟧p)‖1<p2.\\left\\lVert(\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})\\right\\rVert_{1}<\\frac{p}{2}.\n\nMore precisely, the procedure succeeds with probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}, which only depends on the random choice of integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}.\n\nObserve, that the algorithm for[Theorem1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)follows easily with the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}in hand. Namely, considerℳp⋅e⊺=t\\mathcal{M}_{p}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}^{\\intercal}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}, wheret≔(⟦μ1​T⟧p,…,⟦μn​T⟧p)⊺{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}\\coloneqq(\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p})^{\\intercal}. First, because theℓ1\\ell_{1}norm of each row ofℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}is bounded byp/2p/2, vectoreis also a solution to⟨a,e⟩=T\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\rangle=T. Second, by the fact thatℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full-rank, such a vectoreis*unique*. Hence, it is enough to compute the inverse ofℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}(which is possible becauseℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full-rank) and verify that the vector(ℳp)−1⋅t(\\mathcal{M}_{p})^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}is in{0,1}n\\{0,1\\}^{n}(see[Section4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4)for the full proof).\n\nNow, we briefly sketch the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). Our algorithm is inspired by Howgrave-Graham’s revisit[[25](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib25)]of Coppersmith’s method for finding short roots of univariate modular equations[[16](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib16)]. In a nutshell, we construct a lattice (very similar to the original lattice of Lagarias and Odlyzko) for Subset Sum modulo a prime numberpp. Analogously to the original approach of Lagarias and Odlyzko we bound the length of vectors in this lattice. The caveat is that we can also bound the lengths of the vectors in the*dual*lattice. This crucially allows us to double the density of the admissible instances.\n\nThere are two main differences between our proposal and Howgrave-Graham’s algorithm[[25](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib25)](and the original Coppersmith’s method[[16](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib16)]) Firstly, the number of variables to consider in our case is much larger. Secondly, we only need to construct linear polynomials in these variables rather than considering large degree polynomials.\n\n### 1.2Extension to Block Basis Reduction\n\nWe would like to comment on the possible improvements of using stronger lattice reduction. Specifically, the only aspect of the Lagarias-Odlyzko algorithm that relies on the LLL algorithm is the fact that it provides a(γLLL)n(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}-approximate solution to the Shortest Vector Problem withγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}. Instead of the LLL algorithm, it is natural to apply block-basis reduction (e.g.,[[43](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib43)]) with parameterΥ\\Upsilonarbitrarily close to11. This way, one expects a better approximation of SVP, consequently improving the rangeΓ\\Gamma. The obvious drawback of this is the increased running time.\n\nOur approach, in principle, should allow us to reduce the bitlengths of the Subset Sum instances that can be solved regardless of the concrete algorithm behind the lattice reduction. Unfortunately, adapting our approach to work with every basis lattice reduction requires a separate analysis which is much more technical than for LLL. Nevertheless, the takeaway message is as follows:\n\nThe Modular Arithmetic Approach allows us to solve almost all instances of Subset Sum with integersΩ⁡(Γ)\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\Gamma})with a single call to lattice reduction, whereas the previous approach guaranteed to solve only instances with integersΩ⁡(Γ)\\Omega(\\Gamma)while using the same lattice reduction algorithm.\n\nSince the LLL algorithm is the most popular we decided first to present our algorithms using this algorithm. In[Section5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S5), we demonstrate that our approach works for the textbook block-reduction presented by Gama and Nguyen[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)].\n\n###### Theorem 1.3.\n\nLetΥ>1\\Upsilon>1be a parameter of lattice reduction in[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)]. Leta1,…,an∈ℤa_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}be positive integers, selected uniformly at random from range{1,…,⌈Γ⌉}\\{1,\\ldots,\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\Gamma}\\right\\rceil\\}, whereΓ=Υn2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma=\\Upsilon^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}. With overwhelming probability, after a single call to a basis reduction algorithm in[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)]with parameterΥ\\Upsilon, we obtain a polynomial time tester, that for every givenT∈ℤT\\in\\mathbb{Z}decides whetherthere exist​e1,…,en∈{0,1}​such that​∑i=1nei⋅ai=T,\\text{there exist }e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ such that }\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}\\cdot a_{i}=T,\n\nOur tester and its creation method are both deterministic. Their success probability only depends on the random choice of the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}and is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nIn comparison, the standard analysis of Lagarias-Odlyzko algorithm requires the range of integers to be of orderΓ=Υn2⋅2Θ⁡(n​log⁡n)\\Gamma=\\Upsilon^{n^{2}}\\cdot 2^{\\Theta(n\\log{n})}when the lattice reduction with parameterΥ>1\\Upsilon>1is used.\n\n### 1.3Related Work\n\nSubset Sum is an NP-hard problem, closely related to knapsack. The currently fastest algorithm to solve the problem exactly in the worst-case settings runs in𝒪⋆​(2n/2)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{n/2})time[[24](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib24)]. Schroeppel and Shamir[[45](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib45)]show that Subset Sum admits a time-space tradeoff, i.e., an algorithm using𝒮\\mathcal{S}space and2n/𝒮22^{n}/\\mathcal{S}^{2}time for any𝒮⩽𝒪⋆​(2n/4)\\mathcal{S}\\leqslant\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{n/4}). This tradeoff was improved by[[4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib4)]for almost all tradeoff parameters (see also[[18](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib18),[37](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib37)]). In the average-case setting, whend=Θ⁡(1)d=\\Theta(1), Howgrave-Graham and Joux[[26](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib26)]showed𝒪⋆​(20.337​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.337n})time and𝒪⋆​(20.256​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.256n})space algorithm for Subset Sum. This was subsequently improved by Becker, Coron and Joux[[8](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib8)]to𝒪⋆​(20.291​n)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(2^{0.291n})time and space.\n\nThe exact running time of the Subset Sum was also analysed in the parameterized setting[[5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib5),[6](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib6)], quantum regime[[3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib3),[23](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib23)], the polynomial space regime[[7](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib7)]. From the perspective of pseudopolynomial algorithms, Subset Sum has also been the subject of recent stimulative research[[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib12),[30](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib30),[1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib1),[40](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib40),[34](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib34)].\n\n## 2Preliminaries\n\nThrough the paper, we let[n]≔{1,…,n}[n]\\coloneqq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}. We use≡p\\equiv_{p}to denote equivalence modulopp, i.e.,a≡pba\\equiv_{p}biff there existsk∈ℤk\\in\\mathbb{Z}such thata=k⋅p+ba=k\\cdot p+b. For integersa,ba,bwe let⟦a⟧b\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{b}be the unique value in set{−⌈b/2⌉+1,…,⌊b/2⌋}\\{-\\left\\lceil b/2\\right\\rceil+1,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor b/2\\right\\rfloor\\}such that⟦a⟧b≡ba\\llbracket a\\rrbracket_{b}\\equiv_{b}a.𝒪⋆​(⋅)\\mathcal{O}^{\\star}(\\cdot)notation hides factors polynomial innn.\n\nWe considernwith the usual Euclidean topology. We use bold letters to denote vectors. The inner product of two vectorsx=(xi)i=1n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}=(x_{i})_{i=1}^{n}andy=(yi)i=1n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}=(y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}is denoted by⟨x,y⟩=∑i=1nxi⋅yi\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\\cdot y_{i}. We denote theℓp\\ell_{p}norm as‖x‖p≔(∑i=1n(xi)p)1/p\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert_{p}\\coloneqq(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i})^{p})^{1/p}. Often, we omit the subscriptppwhich means that we consider the Euclidean case withp=2p=2. We denote thedd-dimensional Euclidean ball of radiusRRcentered at0byBalld​(R)\\mathrm{Ball}_{d}(R). We use the Stirling formula to approximate the volume of thedd-dimensional ball, namely:Vol⁡(Balld​(R))∼1d​π​(2​π​ed)d/2⋅Rd=2−Θ⁡(d​log⁡(d))⋅Rd.\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathrm{Ball}_{d}(R))\\sim\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d\\pi}}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi e}{d}\\right)^{d/2}\\cdot R^{d}=2^{-\\Theta(d\\log(d))}\\cdot R^{d}.\n\n### 2.1Background on Lattices\n\nA*lattice*innis a discrete subgroup of(n,+)(\\real^{n},+). This implies that a lattice is a non-empty setL⊆nL\\subseteq\\real^{n}such that for allx,y∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in Lit holds thatx−y∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}-{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in L. Any subgroup of(ℤn,+)(\\mathbb{Z}^{n},+)is a lattice, and it is called an*integral lattice*.\n\nLetb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}be vectors inn. Letℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})be the set of integral combinations of these vectors, namely:ℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)≔{∑i=1mℓi⋅bi​such that​ℓ1,…,ℓm∈ℤ}.\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})\\coloneqq\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\ell_{i}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\text{ such that }\\ell_{1},\\ldots,\\ell_{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}.\n\nThen,ℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})is a lattice, specifically the lattice spanned byb1,…,bm.{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}.Furthermore, when the vectorsb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}are linearly independent (over ), we say that they form a basis ofℒ⁡(b1,…,bm).\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}).In this case, every vectorv∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in Luniquely decomposes as an integral combination of the basis vectors , i.e., for everyv∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in Lthere exists uniqueα1,…,αm∈ℤ\\alpha_{1},\\ldots,\\alpha_{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such thatv=∑i=1mαi⋅bi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\alpha_{i}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}[[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38), Definition 5, p. 26]. It is well-known that every lattice admits basis. The cardinality of any basis is an invariant of the lattice and is called its*dimension*(or sometimes referred to as its rank). This quantity, denoted bydim⁡(L)\\mathrm{dim}(L)is equal to the dimension of its linear span (denotedspan⁡(L)\\mathrm{span}(L)) as a vector space over.\n\nWe say that a latticeL⊆nL\\subseteq\\real^{n}has full rank whenn=dim⁡(L)n=\\mathrm{dim}(L). Since any lattice ofnadmits some basis, we assume w.l.o.g. that every lattice we consider is given in the formℒ⁡(b1,…,bm)\\mathcal{L}({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})for somebi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}’s innwherem=dim⁡(L)m=\\mathrm{dim}(L).\n\nThus,b1,…,bm∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}are linearly independent vectors. Their Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization (GSO) is the orthogonal family(b1∗,…,bm∗)({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{m})defined inductively as follows:\n\nb1∗=b1and\\displaystyle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}\\quad\\text{and}bi∗=bi−∑j=1i−1μi,jbj∗whereμi,j≔⟨bi,bj∗⟩‖bj∗‖2for all1⩽j<i⩽m.\\displaystyle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}={\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}-\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}\\mu_{i,j}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\;\\text{ where }\\;\\mu_{i,j}\\coloneqq\\frac{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\rangle}{\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{j}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}}\\;\\text{ for all }\\;1\\leqslant j<i\\leqslant m.\n\nWe can define the volume of the latticeLLspanned byb1,…,bm{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}asVol⁡(L)=∏i=1m‖bi∗‖,\\mathrm{Vol}(L)=\\prod_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert,\n\nand remark that it is a lattice invariant and does not depend on the choice of basis.\n\nOther classical invariants of a lattice are its successive minima. By definition, theiith successive minimum, denoted asλi​(L)\\lambda_{i}(L)is the smallest real number for which there existiilinearly independent vectors inLLeach of length at mostλi​(L)\\lambda_{i}(L). In particular,λ1​(L)\\lambda_{1}(L)is the length of the shortest nonzero vector inLL. Note thatλ1​(L)⩽λ2​(L)⩽…⩽λm​(L)\\lambda_{1}(L)\\leqslant\\lambda_{2}(L)\\leqslant\\ldots\\leqslant\\lambda_{m}(L).\n\n### 2.2Dual Lattice\n\nFor a latticeL⊂nL\\subset\\real^{n}the*dual*lattice ofLLis defined as:L†≔{y∈span(L)such that⟨x,y⟩∈ℤfor allx∈L}.L^{\\dagger}\\coloneqq\\{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}\\in\\mathrm{span}(L)\\text{ such that }\\langle{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{y}}}}\\rangle\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\text{ for all }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in L\\}.\n\nWhenLLis anmm-dimensional lattice ofℝn\\mathbb{R}^{n}thenL†L^{\\dagger}is also anmm-dimensional lattice ofℝn\\mathbb{R}^{n}. More precisely, ifLLis spanned by the rows of the basis matrixBB, thenL†L^{\\dagger}is spanned by the rows of:B†=(B​B⊺)−1​BB^{\\dagger}=\\left(BB^{\\intercal}\\right)^{-1}B\n\nFurthermore, it holds thatVol⁡(L)⋅Vol⁡(L†)=1\\mathrm{Vol}(L)\\cdot\\mathrm{Vol}(L^{\\dagger})=1. Finally, if we reverse the order of the rows ofB†B^{\\dagger}, we obtain the so-called dual basisD†D^{\\dagger}ofBB. The GSO of dual basis is related to the GSO of the primal basis by the following relations:‖bi∗‖⋅‖dm−i+1∗‖=1for every​i∈[m].\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{d}}}_{m-i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert=1\\quad\\mbox{for every}\\ i\\in[m].\n\nIn order to make explicit that the order of the (row) basis vectors is reversed, we write:D†=Rev​((B​B⊺)−1​B).D^{\\dagger}=\\textbf{Rev}\\left(\\left(BB^{\\intercal}\\right)^{-1}B\\right).\n\n### 2.3The LLL algorithm and its property\n\nLenstra, Lenstra and Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]presented a polynomial time procedure that, given an arbitrary basis of a lattice computes a so-called reduced basis with better properties. Extensions of LLL can also compute a reduced basis from an arbitrary generating family.\n\nThe main parameter of LLL is a realδ∈(0.25,1)\\delta\\in(0.25,1). A secondary parameterμ∈[0.5,1)\\mu\\in[0.5,1)is also useful when considering implementations of LLL with finite precision on orthogonalised vectors. The initial LLL with exact rational arithmetic[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]usesμ=1/2\\mu=1/2. Letb1,…,bm∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}be basis of latticeLLandb1∗,…,b∗m∈n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}^{\\ast}_{m}\\in\\real^{n}be its Gram-Schmidt orthogonalized basis ofLL. We say that(b1,…,bm)({\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{m})is*(δ,μ)(\\delta,\\mu)-LLL reduced*if and only if the following conditions are satisfied:Size Reduction:|μi,k|⩽μ\\displaystyle|\\mu_{i,k}|\\leqslant\\mufor every​1⩽k<i⩽m\\displaystyle\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant k<i\\leqslant mLovász Condition:δ​‖bi∗‖2⩽‖bi+1∗‖2+μi+1,i2​‖bi∗‖2\\displaystyle\\delta\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}\\leqslant\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}+\\mu_{i+1,i}^{2}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{2}for every​1⩽i<m\\displaystyle\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant i<m\n\nRecall thatμi,k=⟨bi,bk∗⟩⟨bk∗,bk∗⟩\\mu_{i,k}=\\frac{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\rangle}{\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\rangle}. Both of these conditions yield an*implied parameter*γLLL≔1δ−μ2\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}\\coloneqq\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\delta-\\mu^{2}}}, such that:\n\n‖bi∗‖⩽γLLL⋅‖bi+1∗‖for every1⩽i<m\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i+1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\text{ for every }1\\leqslant i<m(1)\n\n[Equation1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)is the strongest forγLLL=4/3+ε\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}=\\sqrt{4/3}+\\varepsilonfor someε>0\\varepsilon>0. This is achieved by takingμ\\musufficiently close to1/21/2andδ\\deltasufficiently close to11. In the sequel, we focus on the parameterγLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}and express our results in terms of it. Note, that the same inequality holds for the dual lattice.\n\nThe running time of LLL algorithm[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]is𝒪​(m5​n⋅polylog​(B))\\mathcal{O}(m^{5}n\\cdot\\text{polylog}(B)), whereBBis the Euclidean length of the longest basis)[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33),[38](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib38)](see[[42](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib42)]for recent development).\n\n## 3State-of-the-art: Lagarias-Odlyzko Algorithm\n\nAssume thata=(a1,…,an){\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}=(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})is a vector inℤn\\mathbb{Z}^{n}where eachaia_{i}is chosen independently uniformly at random from{1,…,Γ}\\{1,\\ldots,\\Gamma\\}ande=(e1,…,en)∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}is chosen independently at random. SetT=⟨a,e⟩T=\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\rangle. Then, clearlyeis a solution to the equation:∑i=1nai​xi=T​such that​xi∈{0,1}​for every​i∈{1,…,n}.\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}x_{i}=T\\text{ such that }x_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ for every }i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}.\n\nHere, we recall the argument of Lagarias and Odlyzko who show that ifΓ⩾Ω⁡((γLLL)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma\\geqslant\\Omega((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})})andγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}, then the LLL algorithm is sufficient to solve an instancea1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}with targetTTof Subset Sum in polynomial time and with probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nNote thatT⩽∑i=1naiT\\leqslant\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}, and we can assume that‖e‖1⩽n2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant\\frac{n}{2}as otherwise, we can consider the same instance with target(∑i=1nai−T)\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}-T\\right). LetK≔⌈n​(γLLL)n⌉K\\coloneqq\\left\\lceil n(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\right\\rceilbe a sufficiently large integer. Lagarias and Odlyzko[[31](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib31)]construct the following row-generated lattice:\n\nL≔(10⋯0a1⋅K01⋯0a2⋅K⋱00⋯1an⋅K00⋯0T⋅K).L\\coloneqq\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&\\cdots&0&a_{1}\\cdot K\\\\ 0&1&\\cdots&0&a_{2}\\cdot K\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&\\cdots&1&a_{n}\\cdot K\\\\ 0&0&\\cdots&0&T\\cdot K\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nLete¯=(e1,…,en,0)\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}=(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n},0)such that∑i=1nei​ai=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}a_{i}=T. Observe thate¯∈L\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\in Land‖e¯‖⩽n/2\\left\\lVert\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\sqrt{n/2}. Therefore, the vectore¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}is a*short vector*ofLL.\n\nThe idea of Lagarias and Odlyzko is to use the algorithm of Lenstra, Lenstra and Lovász[[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib33)]. Letvbe a minimum length non-zero vector inLL. The LLL algorithm guarantees finding a nonzero vectorx∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in Lwith:‖x‖⩽(γLLL)n​‖v‖.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\right\\rVert.\n\nHere,γLLL\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}is an implied parameter of the LLL algorithm, and we can guarantee that whenγLLL>4/3\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}}>\\sqrt{4/3}, the LLL algorithm runs in polynomial time (see[Section2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2)). Note that the vectorxfound by the LLL satisfies‖x‖⩽(γLLL)n​‖e¯‖⩽(γLLL)n​n/2<K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\left\\lVert\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n}\\sqrt{n/2}<K\n\nThe strategy is, hence, to run the LLL algorithm and hope that the vectorxreturned by LLL is eithere¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}or−e¯-\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}. There is of course the possibility that there are other spurious vectors of length less thanKK. The analysis by Lagarias and Odlyzko demonstrates that this is, however, highly unlikely. More precisely, they show the following statement:\n\n###### Claim 3.1(cf.,[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]).\n\nℙ𝕣[existsx∈L∖{k⋅e∣k∈ℤ}such that‖x‖<K]⩽(2​K+1)n+1Γ.\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[\\text{exists }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\in L\\setminus\\{k\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\mid k\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\}\\text{ such that }\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{x}}}\\right\\rVert<K\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{(2K+1)^{n+1}}{\\Gamma}.\n\nNote that this is2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}whenΓ⩾Ω⁡((γLLL)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma\\geqslant\\Omega((\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})}). Therefore, by considering the opposite event, we can guarantee that the vectore¯\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}is found with a probability at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}. We include the proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)due to[[19](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib19)]as it serves as an introduction to our technique.\n\n###### Proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1).\n\nLetw=(w1,…,wn+1)∈L{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}=(w_{1},\\ldots,w_{n+1})\\in L. Observe that ifwn+1≠0w_{n+1}\\neq 0then already‖w‖⩾K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert\\geqslant K. Hence, it must hold thatwn+1=0w_{n+1}=0. Let𝒲≔{w∈Lsuch that‖w‖<Kandwn+1=0andw≠k⋅e¯for anyk∈ℤ}.\\mathcal{W}\\coloneqq\\{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\in L\\text{ such that }\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert<K\\text{ and }w_{n+1}=0\\text{ and }{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\neq k\\cdot\\bar{{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}}\\text{ for any }k\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\}.\n\nNow, it suffices to bound the probability that𝒲\\mathcal{W}is empty. If, however,𝒲\\mathcal{W}is nonempty, there existsw=(w1,…,wn,0)∈ℤn+1{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}=(w_{1},\\ldots,w_{n},0)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}andℓ∈ℤ\\ell\\in\\mathbb{Z}that satisfy:∑i=1nai⋅wi=ℓ⋅T.\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\\cdot w_{i}=\\ell\\cdot T.(2)\n\nLet us now fix this vectorwand integerℓ\\ell. Letz=(z1,…,zn)∈ℤn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}=(z_{1},\\ldots,z_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n}be such thatzi=wi−ei⋅ℓz_{i}=w_{i}-e_{i}\\cdot\\ell. Observe that⟨a,z⟩=0\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}\\rangle=0. Without loss of generality, we can assume thatz1≠0z_{1}\\neq 0and we letZ≔−(∑i=2nai​zi/z1)Z\\coloneqq-(\\sum_{i=2}^{n}a_{i}z_{i}/z_{1}). Hence,ℙ𝕣[⟨a,z⟩=0]=ℙ𝕣[a1=Z]=∑i=1Γℙ𝕣[a1=i|Z=i]⋅ℙ𝕣[Z=i].\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[\\langle{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}},{\\mathrm{\\textbf{z}}}\\rangle=0\\right]=\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[a_{1}=Z\\right]=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\Gamma}\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[a_{1}=i\\;|\\;Z=i\\right]\\cdot\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[Z=i\\right].\n\nAsa1a_{1}andZZare independent, this is bounded by∑i=1Γ1Γ⋅ℙ𝕣[Z=i]⩽1Γ.\\sum_{i=1}^{\\Gamma}\\frac{1}{\\Gamma}\\cdot\\mathbb{Pr}\\left[Z=i\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{1}{\\Gamma}.\n\nTherefore, a fixedwandℓ\\ellsatisfy Equation ([2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.E2)) with probability at most1/Γ1/\\Gamma. Note that the number ofw∈ℤn+1{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}andℓ∈ℤ\\ell\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that‖w‖<K\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{w}}}\\right\\rVert<K,wn+1=0w_{n+1}=0and|ℓ|⩽2​K|\\ell|\\leqslant 2Kis at most(2​K+1)n+1(2K+1)^{n+1}and the proof concludes. ∎\n\nNote, that the proof of[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)uses lattice reduction as a blackbox. If one were to use lattice reduction with parameterΥ>1\\Upsilon>1, then the range guaranteed by[3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S3.Thmtheorem1)would beΓ=Θ⁡((Υ)n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma=\\Theta((\\Upsilon)^{n^{2}+o(n^{2})}). In the next sections, we will show a method to improve the admissible range down toΓ=Θ⁡((Υ)0.5​n2+o⁡(n2))\\Gamma=\\Theta((\\Upsilon)^{0.5n^{2}+o(n^{2})})that uses more properties of lattice reduction. In[Section4](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4)we analyse it with the standard LLL. Then in section[Section5](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S5)we analyse it with a textbook block lattice reduction of Gama and Nguyen[[22](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#bib.bib22)].\n\n## 4Modular arithmetic approach\n\nIn this section, we focus on the proof of the following statement.\n\nSee[1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)\n\nBefore we do so, let us show how to use[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)to solve the Subset Sum formally.\n\n###### Proof of Theorem[1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem1)assuming[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\nWe start by describing the algorithm. First, we use[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)to compute the integersμ1,…,μn,p\\mu_{1},\\ldots,\\mu_{n},p. This gives us the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}. We know that this matrix has full rank so we can compute its inverse with the Gaussian elimination algorithm. This concludes the description of the preprocessing phase.\n\nNow, given a targetTTwe compute the vectortp≔(⟦μ1​T⟧p,…,⟦μn​T⟧p)⊺{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}\\coloneqq(\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p})^{\\intercal}and compute a candidate solutione⊺=ℳp−1⋅tp{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}^{\\intercal}=\\mathcal{M}_{p}^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}. Finally, we check thateis indeed correct. Namely, if∑i=1nei​aj=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}e_{i}a_{j}=Tande∈{0,1}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}we returneand⊥\\bototherwise. This concludes the description of the algorithm. Clearly, the algorithm runs in polynomial time. Moreover, a query takes only𝒪⁡(n)\\mathcal{O}(n)arithmetic operations (on numbers from the range[R][R]). The success probability of the algorithm comes exclusively from a single application of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). Therefore, it remains to argue about the correctness.\n\nLete1,…,en∈{0,1}ne_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}be a solution to∑i=1nai​ei=T\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}e_{i}=Tand assume that our algorithm returns⊥\\bot. Because the norm‖⟦μi​a⟧p‖1\\left\\lVert\\llbracket\\mu_{i}{\\mathrm{\\textbf{a}}}\\rrbracket_{p}\\right\\rVert_{1}is bounded byp/2p/2it holds that:\n\n(⟦μ1​a1⟧p⟦μ1​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ1​an⟧p⟦μ2​a1⟧p⟦μ2​a2⟧p⋯⟦μ2​an⟧p⟦μn​a1⟧p⟦μn​a2⟧p⋯⟦μn​an⟧p)​(e1e2en)=(⟦μ1​T⟧p⟦μ2​T⟧p⟦μn​T⟧p)\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{1}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{2}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots&&&\\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{2}\\rrbracket_{p}&\\cdots&\\llbracket\\mu_{n}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}e_{1}\\\\ e_{2}\\\\ \\vdots\\\\ e_{n}\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\llbracket\\mu_{1}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{2}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\vdots\\\\ \\llbracket\\mu_{n}T\\rrbracket_{p}\\\\ \\end{pmatrix}\n\nSince the matrixℳp\\mathcal{M}_{p}has full rank, there exists a unique solution to the above linear system. Hence(e1,…,en)⊺=ℳp−1⋅tp(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})^{\\intercal}=\\mathcal{M}_{p}^{-1}\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{t}}}_{p}. Finally, note that we only return this solution if all its coordinates are in{0,1}n\\{0,1\\}^{n}, thus ensuring an incorrect solution is never returned. ∎\n\nFrom now on we will focus on the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\n### 4.1Generating Family\n\nLetp∈ℕp\\in\\mathbb{N}be the prime fixed in[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2). In particular,ppis odd. Because the numbersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}are generated at random in the interval{1,…,ΓLO}\\{1,\\ldots,\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}\\}which has length much larger thanpp, the modular reductionsai(modp)a_{i}\\pmod{p}are very close to uniform modulopp. For simplicity of the analysis we want to assume that they are uniform. This can be achieved by using the rejection sampling to discard any event when at least oneaia_{i}is greater thanσp\\sigma_{p}, whereσp\\sigma_{p}is the largest multiple ofppsuch thatσp<ΓLO\\sigma_{p}<\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}. This is valid, because the probability that specificaia_{i}is discarded is:\n\nℙ[ai>σp]⩽|ΓLO−σp|ΓLO⩽2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n).\\mathbb{P}\\left[a_{i}>\\sigma_{p}\\right]\\leqslant\\frac{|\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}-\\sigma_{p}|}{\\sqrt{\\Gamma_{\\textsf{LO}}}}\\leqslant 2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}.\n\nTherefore, by the union bound, probability that we do not discard any ofa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}is at least1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}. Similarly, we can assume thata1a_{1}is not a multiple ofpp. Hence, there exists an integera1−1a_{1}^{-1}such thata1⋅a1−1≡p1a_{1}\\cdot a_{1}^{-1}\\equiv_{p}1.\n\nConsider the lattice generated by the rows of the following matrix:ℒ=(a1a2a3⋯anp00⋯00p0000p0⋱000…p)∈ℤ(n+1)×n\\mathcal{L}=\\begin{pmatrix}a_{1}&a_{2}&a_{3}&\\cdots&a_{n}\\\\ p&0&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ 0&p&0&&0\\\\ 0&0&p&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&0&\\ldots&p\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{(n+1)\\times n}(3)\n\nNow, let us elaborate on the connection between matrixℒ\\mathcal{L}and any vector(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})from the statement of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2).\n\n###### Observation 4.1.\n\nFor every integerμ∈ℤ\\mu\\in\\mathbb{Z}it holds that:\n\n(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)∈ℒ.(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})\\in\\mathcal{L}.\n\nConversely, for every vectorv∈ℒ∩{−⌊p/2⌋,…,⌊p/2⌋}n{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in\\mathcal{L}\\cap\\{-\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor\\}^{n}, there existsμ′∈ℤ\\mu^{\\prime}\\in\\mathbb{Z}such that:v=(⟦μ′​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ′​an⟧p).{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=(\\llbracket\\mu^{\\prime}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu^{\\prime}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p}).\n\nMoreover,μ′\\mu^{\\prime}is unique moduloppand its exact value can be determined with a constant number of arithmetic operations.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nFor the first property observe that triviallyμ⋅(a1,…,an)∈ℒ\\mu\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})\\in\\mathcal{L}. Letei{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}be the vector with11at theiith coordinate and00at the remaining coordinates. Note that by definition(p⋅ei)∈ℒ(p\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i})\\in\\mathcal{L}for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. Then we construct the desired vector by\n\n(⟦μ​a1⟧p,…,⟦μ​an⟧p)=μ⋅(a1,…,an)−∑i=1n⌊μ​aip⌋⋅ei∈ℒ.(\\llbracket\\mu a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})=\\mu\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\mu a_{i}}{p}\\right\\rfloor\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}\\in\\mathcal{L}.\n\nFor the converse property, by definition any vectorv∈ℒ{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}\\in\\mathcal{L}is represented as:\n\nv=k0⋅(a1,…,an)+∑i=1n(ki​p)⋅ei.{\\mathrm{\\textbf{v}}}=k_{0}\\cdot(a_{1},\\ldots,a_{n})+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(k_{i}p)\\cdot{\\mathrm{\\textbf{e}}}_{i}.\n\nfor some integersk0,…,kn∈ℤk_{0},\\ldots,k_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}. Let us inspect theiith coordinate ofv. For everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]it holds thatk0⋅ai+ki⋅p∈{−⌊p/2⌋,…,⌊p/2⌋}k_{0}\\cdot a_{i}+k_{i}\\cdot p\\in\\{-\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor,\\ldots,\\left\\lfloor p/2\\right\\rfloor\\}. Therefore, for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n], theiith coordinate ofvis⟦k0​ai⟧p\\llbracket k_{0}a_{i}\\rrbracket_{p}. We hence can setμ′≔k0\\mu^{\\prime}\\coloneqq k_{0}. Note, that given vectorv, the integerμ′\\mu^{\\prime}can be computed efficiently because it is expressed asμ′=v1⋅a1−1\\mu^{\\prime}=v_{1}\\cdot a_{1}^{-1}, wherev1v_{1}is the first coordinate ofv. ∎\n\nNow, let us further elaborate on the subsequent steps. We run the LLL algorithm onℒ\\mathcal{L}. The LLL algorithm returns a basisb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}ofℒ\\mathcal{L}. We show that, with high probability,‖bi‖1⩽p/2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant p/2for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. If that occurs we are nearly finished. By the[4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem1)each of the basis vectorsbi{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}is equal to(⟦μi​a1⟧p,…,⟦μi​an⟧p)(\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{1}\\rrbracket_{p},\\ldots,\\llbracket\\mu_{i}a_{n}\\rrbracket_{p})for some integerμi∈ℤ\\mu_{i}\\in\\mathbb{Z}. Moreover, because the vectorsb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}form a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}it means that the matrix formed by the vectorsb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\dots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}has a full-rank.\n\nTherefore, to complete the proof of[Theorem1.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S1.Thmtheorem2)it suffices to show that‖bi‖1⩽p/2\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{1}\\leqslant p/2for everyi∈[n]i\\in[n]. By Cauchy-Schwartz inequality it is actually sufficient to prove that‖bi‖2⩽p2​n\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{i}\\right\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{n}}.\n\n### 4.2Basis of Generating Family and Dual Basis\n\nLatticeℒ\\mathcal{L}is given to us by a generating family in form of a rectangular matrix. It is much more convenient to work with a basis, especially when given by a square matrice. Therefore, we start by determining a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}. As we have already noticed, we can assume without loss of generality thata1a_{1}is invertible inℤp\\mathbb{Z}_{p}. For everyi∈2,…,ni\\in{2,\\ldots,n}, letαi=ai​a1−1\\alpha_{i}=a_{i}a^{-1}_{1}. The rows of the following matrix form a basis ofℒ\\mathcal{L}:\n\nℬ0=(1α2α3⋯αn0p0000p0⋱000…p)∈ℤn×n\\mathcal{B}_{0}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\alpha_{2}&\\alpha_{3}&\\cdots&\\alpha_{n}\\\\ 0&p&0&&0\\\\ 0&0&p&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 0&0&0&\\ldots&p\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n\\times n}\n\nIn particular, this implies that the volume ofℒ\\mathcal{L}ispn−1p^{n-1}. We can determine the dual lattice ofℒ\\mathcal{L}, which in the full-dimensional case is spanned by the rows of the transpose of the inverse matrix. In our case, this is:ℬ0†=(ℬ0−1)T=1p⋅(p00⋯0−α210⋯0−α3010⋱−αn00…1)∈ℚn×n\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{\\dagger}=(\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{-1})^{T}=\\frac{1}{p}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix}p&0&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ -\\alpha_{2}&1&0&\\cdots&0\\\\ -\\alpha_{3}&0&1&&0\\\\ \\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ -\\alpha_{n}&0&0&\\ldots&1\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{Q}^{n\\times n}\n\nObserve thatp​ℬ0†p\\mathcal{B}_{0}^{\\dagger}generates the set of integral vectors(x1,…,xn)∈ℤn(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n}such that∑i=2nxi⋅αi≡p−x1\\sum_{i=2}^{n}x_{i}\\cdot\\alpha_{i}\\equiv_{p}-x_{1}. Or equivalently such that:∑i=1nxiai≡p0.\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}a_{i}\\equiv_{p}0.\n\n### 4.3Every vector of the LLL-reduced basis is probably short\n\nNow, letℬ\\mathcal{B}be an LLL-reduced basis of the latticeℒ\\mathcal{L}. We denote its row vectors byb1,…,bn{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}and their GSO byb1∗,…,bn∗{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast},\\ldots,{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}. Recall that the dual basis, given byℬ†=Rev​((ℬ−1)T)\\mathcal{B}^{\\dagger}=\\textbf{Rev}((\\mathcal{B}^{-1})^{T}).\n\nIn this section, we prove the following probabilistic property on a reduced basis:\n\n###### Lemma 4.2.\n\nWith probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}, it holds that:‖bk∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−12​Vol​(ℒ)1/nfor every​k∈{1,…,n}.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}\\quad\\text{for every}\\ k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}.\n\n[Lemma4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem2)can be proved as a consequence of the following inequalities, which hold with overwhelming probability when the integersa1,…,ana_{1},\\ldots,a_{n}are drawn uniformly at random.\n\n###### Claim 4.3.\n\nWith probability⩾1−2−Ω⁡(n​log⁡n)\\geqslant 1-2^{-\\Omega(n\\log{n})}it holds that:‖bn∗‖⩽Vol​(ℒ)1/n⩽‖b1∗‖.\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}\\leqslant\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert.\n\n###### Proof of[Lemma4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem2)assuming[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3).\n\nLet us assume that both inequalities in[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3)hold. Ifk⩾n+12k\\geqslant\\frac{n+1}{2}then by repeated application of Inequality ([1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)) we have that‖bk∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−k​‖bn∗‖⩽(γLLL)n−12​Vol​(ℒ)1/n\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{n-k}\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{n}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\leqslant(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\mathrm{Vol}(\\mathcal{L})^{1/n}, which is the desired result. Hence we need to focus on the case wherek<n+12k<\\frac{n+1}{2}. Observe that by repeated application of Inequality ([1](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S2.E1)) and[4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2408.16108#S4.Thmtheorem3)we have‖b1∗‖⋯‖bk−1∗‖\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert\\cdots\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{k-1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert⩾∏i=1k−1‖b1∗‖(γLLL)i−1=(γLLL)−(k−1)​(k−2)2⋅‖b1∗‖k−1⩾(γLLL)−(k−1)​(k−2)2⋅Vol​(ℒ)k−1n.\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\prod_{i=1}^{k-1}\\frac{\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{i-1}}=(\\gamma_{\\textsf{LLL}})^{-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}{2}}\\cdot\\left\\lVert{\\mathrm{\\textbf{b}}}_{1}^{\\ast}\\right\\rVer